editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:1:# Chapter 34 — A Good Room
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:1:# Chapter 33 — Fixity
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:173:Brom told her, and she listened. Then she came in, and sat on the floor with her back to the wall, and drew a line on the squared paper at the bell, and wrote beside it, in her wide crooked left-handed letters, *Doorway. Uncommanded. 12 s. B. counting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:1:# Chapter 29 — The Catechism
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:1:# Chapter 30 — The Upper Ferry
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:1:# Chapter 32 — Road Dust
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:1:# Chapter 31 — Four Exchanges
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:1:# Chapter 28 — Where the Mark Sat
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:3:The count was slow, and the four men moved on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:5:Cael had seen drills like it on the second floor, when Rooke wanted a team to learn to arrive. You did not run at your mark. You walked, and the count told you how long you had, and you timed your stride so that you came onto your mark on the last word, at the same moment as everyone else came onto theirs. Four men arriving together round a coach were not four men. They were a ring. A ring could be drawn tight by one more word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:7:The lead did not move. He stayed by the cart's wheel with one hand lifted, keeping the time, and sent the other four in. They came from the two ditches and the two ends, one at each corner of the coach, unhurried, with their staves still at their belts and their hands open. *One.* Their feet touched the chalk at the same instant. *Two.* The lead's voice went along the cut like a man calling a dance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He saw her go out of the corner of his eye, because he was already moving the other way. She did not go at any of them. She went to the place behind the coach where the nearest man's next stride was going to come down, and she was standing in it, feet set, when his foot arrived. He came onto her instead of onto his mark. It was not a blow. It was worse than a blow, for a man in a drill: it was the wrong ground, a beat early, and he stumbled into her and off her and lost the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:15:Her second burst took her across the coach's tail and up the north camber to the ditch side, low, so fast that the dust she left behind her hung in the air in the shape of the road she had taken. The man coming up out of the ditch found her where his third stride should have been and stopped dead with his hands up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:17:The lead's voice did not falter. He said *four*. But there were only two men on their marks for it, and a ring with two men in it is a pair of men.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:19:That was all Cael saw. He did not see the third burst or the fourth. He had his own road to take, and it went straight up the crown at the woman in the hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:25:She stood on the flat patch of crown halfway between the coach's horses and the cart, three-quarters turned, with her hands loose at her sides and her weight even. She was not looking at him. She was looking at a place on the road about a pace in front of him and a little to his left. He did not understand that until later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:27:He went at her because she was the key. Every one of the fourteen was hers. He had felt them through his palm and he had felt whose they were, the way you can tell, without being told, that all the letters in a bundle are in one hand. If she was down, or moving, or frightened, she could not lay a fifteenth. He did not mean to hurt her. He meant to make her move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:31:It was a good burst. He had been holding the band down so long that the full thing came out of him almost with relief: the gather low in the belly, the hip loading like a drawn bow, and the shove at his back. The road came up under him. Six feet out from the place he had left, it stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:33:Not the road. Him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:35:He hit something in the air in front of his chest. It was not a wall, because a wall would have had an edge, and a surface, and a feel. It was a single point, about the height of his breastbone, and it did not give at all. The burst went into it and stopped there, all of it, the way a cart stops when its wheel finds a post in the dark. He went back off it onto his heels. His breath went out of him. The landing beat locked his legs where they stood, the same short pause it had always held him in, and for that pause he could do nothing but stand on the chalk with the point six inches in front of him and know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:37:She had laid it while he was in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:39:He saw now where she had been looking. She had been looking at the place he was going to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:She had not lifted a hand. She had not moved her feet. She had not even turned toward him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:49:That was the first thing. Every fighter he had ever faced had come at him with something, a burst or a course or a lane or a fist. They had put a thing into the space between them and asked him to deal with it. She did the opposite. She took the space away. Every step he meant to take, she got there first and took the place he meant to put his foot, and left him with one less step in the world than he had had a moment before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:51:She was reading him. That was the second thing, and it made him cold all down his back. In the outer court he had watched her get onto a horse without the half beat, the little gathering of the body that tells you a person has decided to move. Somebody had trained it out of her. And because she had none, she could see it in everybody else. She saw his half beat. She saw where his weight was going before his weight knew it. She laid her point there and waited for him to arrive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:53:Somewhere at the back of him, in its sealed place, the quiet thing from the stair stirred, the way it always stirred when he was cornered, and offered to take him out of every eye in the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:55:He did not look at it. It stayed where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:59:He turned his head for the length of one breath. Two of the four had given up on the ring. They had seen where their work was, and it was not at the coach. It was in the channel along the south bank, under the lip, where the frame left its one way open to the culvert, and where Brom was standing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:61:The first of them had gone at him square. He was a heavy man, nearly Brom's height, and he had come down the channel at a run with his shoulder low. Brom had not stepped into him or away from him. He had simply been there, where the man was going, with his feet set and his arms loose and his chin in. The man had arrived. The sound had been the man arriving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:65:That was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:67:Cael turned back to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:71:He found that out when he went to take a third, and his heel came down against air. There was a point behind him now, at the height of his calf, where there had been nothing when he walked through that place. She had laid it while he was looking at Brom. He had not even felt her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:75:Every point she had laid since he came at her was still there. The one at his breastbone. The one under his left foot. The one behind his calf. He could not see any of them. He could feel each one if he moved toward it, the way you feel furniture in a dark room by walking into it. And she was not lifting them to lay new ones. She was laying new ones as well. The space round him was the space round him minus three, and in a moment it would be minus four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:77:She did not need to hurry. She had all morning. She had laid fourteen before he ever came into the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:85:He did. He went backward off his own push as if he had shoved at the side of a house. It threw him two yards along the crown to the north, faster than he could have gone on his feet, and that was useful. For a moment he was out of the pocket she had been building round him. He took it. He used the second push the same way, against the same point from a new side, and it threw him again, west, toward the coach. The third threw him back east. The point stayed where it was every time. It was the most solid thing on the road. He was using it the way a swimmer uses the side of a bath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:91:Four pushes and she had not taken a step. Four pushes and he was still in the cut, with three points round him and the woman on her flat patch of crown, and every push had moved nobody but him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:95:He went wide, fast, toward the north ditch, as if he meant to run along it past her to the cart. She laid a point in front of him at the ditch's edge. It was further from her than any she had laid yet, and he saw what it cost her. She was still for an instant. Not the stillness of a woman waiting. The stillness of a woman doing something, the way a person goes still to thread a needle. Half a second. Less. Then she was waiting again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:101:He went wide a third time, north again, and she did not lay anything at all. She simply watched him go, and when he turned to come back, the place he was turning into had a point in it, close to her, cheap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:103:She had learned him faster than he had learned her. She had stopped biting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:107:He had a moment, then, because she let him have it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:111:She was at the edge of the north ditch, where the camber fell away into it, sitting in the chalk with one leg out in front of her and her hands flat behind her. She had spent four bursts. He had not seen the third or the fourth, and he did not know which had put her there. He knew only that her right foot was turned at an angle no foot was meant to hold, and that she was holding very still. One of the four stood over her, a yard off, with his stave across his body in both hands. He did not touch her. He did not need to. He stood between her and the coach and looked at her, and she looked back at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:113:Custody. Not assault. They did not want her. They wanted her out of the way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:117:He knew what she was doing as soon as he saw it, because he had heard her explain the difference on the night of the gate, counting the words off on her fingers. Ternhall's anchors were her own, three footholds that drank the heat of a contact down into a floor. The Lattice Path was lines strung across the air. These were neither. These were points, and they held. But something ran between them. When she had put her hand out to the horse's leg she had found it, the way you find a rope across a dark yard: a line, taut, between the point fixed against the horse's foreleg and one of the fourteen in the bank. And she had put her heat into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:121:He could not see it. He saw the horse's leg shift, a finger's breadth, as if somebody had cut one strand of a halter. He saw Karis's face, set and white, and her right hand, and the skin of her fingers going red from the tips down. She moved the hand an inch along the line she could not see and put her heat into it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:131:Brom took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:133:Cael heard it. He heard Iron Skin hold, a flat dense sound like a hammer on an anvil with a cloth over it. And under that sound, smaller, he heard something else, a click and a give, and saw Brom's left arm drop an inch at the elbow and not come back. Brom's face did not change. His feet did not move. The man came off him and went down on the chalk and stayed down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:135:That was two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:137:Brom stood in the channel with his left arm hanging a little and looked at the culvert, and the gully, and the cart. He did not look at his shoulder. He had told the kitchen once that a man who looks at a hurt only teaches it to be looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:145:He would put it down the north side of the crown, past her, along the camber, from where he stood to the cart, fifteen feet of leaning air. She could lay her points where he was going to be. Let her try it at the speed a lane went. He would be past her before she had finished threading the needle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:149:It leaned. He felt the whole cut give a little along one line, as a board gives when you step on its end. His ears closed and opened. The chalk dust woke up on the crown and ran in a thin white stream along the line he had laid, east, past her, toward the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:151:And the read went out of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:153:He had known it would. It was the rule. At Gold weight the read took the air or the body, one or the other, never both; you laid the lane first and then you gave the read back, and it came back to you in a breath or two if you let it. He had no breath or two. He stepped into the lane's downhill with the read still out in the air somewhere and nothing to tell him where her points were but his own memory of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:155:The lane carried him. The road came at him faster than his feet had any right to bring it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:159:He did not feel it until he was in it. He went into it at the lane's full speed on the right side, low, and the whole of his weight and the whole of the lane's went into one place at the bottom of his ribs. It did not give. He did. He heard himself make a sound. Then he was off the lane and off his feet and rolling, across the crown, down the south camber, over and over, with the chalk and the grit coming up at him white, and he put his left arm out to stop himself because there was nothing else to put out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:161:The road took the skin off it. He felt it go from the wrist to the elbow, a long hot scrape, as if somebody had drawn a rasp up the inside of his forearm. He came to rest on his side at the bottom of the camber, a few yards short of the channel, with his face in the dust and his right side one solid ache.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:163:And then the bill came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:167:One.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:Two. Another, at his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:177:He lay there and counted them in as they came, one for each of his slow counts, because counting was the only thing left that came when he called. Five. Six. She was not hurrying even now. She was laying them the way a mason lays a course of bricks, each one on the last, close round him, closer. Seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:183:Nine. Ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:Eleven, and the bill was paid, and everything answered when he called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:There was a cage round him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:193:He felt every bar of it. They were not the fourteen; the fourteen were still out in the cut where they had always been, at the mouths and in the banks and on the milestone. These were new. They were close. They stood round him at the height of his knees and his shoulders and his head, a ring of points two feet off on every side, and above him, and on the chalk under him. He counted them twice. Nineteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:199:He had been carrying it since the first exchange. He understood that now, kneeling. When his chest had found the point at his breastbone she had not only stopped him. She had laid a second point against him, against the cloth over Hesk's book, so lightly that he had taken it for the bruise of hitting the first one. It went where he went. It had gone with him on every push and every turn and down the camber. It was the first point of the cage, and every other point in the cage was laid from it, and to it, and through it, the way every stitch in a row of knitting is worked through the stitch before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:201:He could feel the lines now, faintly, through the read. They ran from the point on his coat out to the nineteen round him, and from the nineteen out to the fourteen in the cut, and from all of them, all of them, back to her. She stood on her flat patch of crown fifteen feet away with every line in the cut running into her two loose hands like the reins of a team.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:203:Three more, and it would close. He could feel exactly where. Three gaps, one at his left shoulder, one at his right hip, one over his head. When those three were laid, the cage would stop being a cage and start being a hold. A person inside a closed hold did not walk to a culvert. He was carried there, quite gently, by people who did not need to touch him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:209:*One. The conditions are not to be made.* He had not made this road. The river had made it, and somebody who read rivers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:211:*Two. A reach only inside a fight the other begins, in earnest, awake, and on purpose.* She had begun it. She had been beginning it since before the coach came out of the ferry. Nobody had ever been more in earnest. And he was awake, as awake as he had ever been, on his knees in the white dust with his arm on fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:He knelt in the cage and held still, all of him, in front of four exchanges of her. That was all it had ever been. Not taking anything. He turned everything he had left toward what he had watched her do, and kept it there, open. Subtraction. The points laid where his half beat said he would go. The stillness of a woman threading a needle at the edge of her reach, half a second's price for range. The accumulation that never gave anything back. The stitch against his coat, and everything worked through it. He had paid for every one of those in his own body on this road, and he knew them now as well as he knew the stair to his own room with the lamps out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:221:It was quiet. It did not come down on him like the storm had come, all at once and too heavy. It came in like a key going into a lock it had been cut for. And then he could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:Every course in the cut stood out to him as plainly as the chalk dust had stood out along his lane: taut grey threads in the air, from point to point to point, a great slack net laid down the whole length of the road, closed at both ends, open in one channel where Brom stood. He saw the nineteen round him, each with its line to the next. He saw the one against his coat, the first stitch, with every other line in the cage worked through it. And he saw how it was held. It was held from inside, from where it touched him. It was the same thing as the point at his breastbone and the one under the horse's leg and the ones that held the coachman's hands. It was a thing set against a surface and told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:227:From inside, he could tell it not to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:229:He let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:231:It went the way you let go of a breath you have been holding without knowing it. The point against his coat was simply not there any more. And because every other point in the cage had been laid through it, they went after it, one after another, in the order she had laid them, unthreading round him in a quick soft ripple like a row of stitches coming out when the first is pulled. The nineteen went. Their lines went out to the fourteen in the cut, and the fourteen went too. He felt them go down the road behind him like a rope paying out over a wheel: the three across the west mouth, the two in the south bank's lip, the three high in the north bank, the parapets, the milestone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:239:He saw that. He was still on his knees and he saw her feel her whole morning go out through her fingers, fourteen points laid before light and every one she had laid since, all of them at once. She did not stagger. She did not make a sound. Her two loose hands closed, slowly, as if somebody had pulled a rope out of them faster than she could hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:245:He counted them, because it was the last thing he could do. On one the man standing over Lira turned his back on her and went for the north bank, and the one at the off horse's head let go of his stave with one hand and went after him at a run. On two the two men in the channel were on their feet and going, the second holding his own arm, past Brom, who let them past, into the gully beyond the culvert. On three the man at the cart was gone behind it, and the ropes on the north bank were taking weight. On four there was nobody on the road at all but the four of them and the coachman and the horses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:247:And her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:249:She was last. She stood on her flat patch of crown for one more moment, with her hands closed, and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:251:It lasted one second. He could see nothing of her face but the band of skin between the hood and the scarf and the two eyes in it. They were grey, and steady, and not angry. He had braced for anger. It was not there. It was the look of somebody who has spent her whole life on one craft and has just watched it done backward, in front of her, by a boy on his knees in the dust, and is trying to see how.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:259:The minute was coming. Cael could feel it coming, everything in him beginning to lean toward the new thing, as a crowded room leans toward somebody who has just come in. He did not try to stand. He knelt in the white dust and let it come, and Brom got there first, and stood over him, and did not say anything at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:7:*MOULD MENDED THIS YEAR. WIRE WENT AT THE TOP OF THE MARK. SEWED IT MYSELF. A SIXTEENTH LOW, I KNEW IT WOULD BE, THERE WAS NO HELPING IT. ONE RUN OFF IT SINCE FOR THE REGISTRY. WE CALL IT THE SECOND. PAID IN FULL.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:9:The last three words were the part Seln read twice. The man had added them on his own. He had understood exactly what was being spent, and he wanted it known that the account was closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:11:Seln put the circular in the copying-room stove and watched the pencil go brown and then go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:13:One favour, one fact. The paper that had come up the hill to the gate had been made this year, off a mended mould, in the south mill's second run of registry stock. That was all: it did not say who had drawn it, or where it had gone. It only said that somebody, somewhere, after the mending, had ordered registry paper and been sent it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:21:*In the office column a mark where the word should be. A mark, not a word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:27:It was the Fourth-day. The porter's shoulder was still strapped, so the sort was still his, and he had liked it since the first morning. The post room behind the records hall's counter was a narrow place with forty pigeonholes along one wall, a bench along the other, a brass beam for weighing parcels and the stove the porter kept going from the first bell whatever the weather. In half an hour at that bench you knew where every letter in the house was going and roughly why, and nobody watched you learn it. It was the most honest half-hour of his day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:A mould mended in the south, and a second run off it this year. Thirteen honest lots, gone out in the ordinary way to offices with names and doors and clerks who could be written to. And a fourteenth, the same week, off the same run, on the same stock, that had not gone into the ledger at all but over it. Written in above the line by somebody who could reach a store-house ledger in the west and open it and put a thing where nothing went, and leave a mark in the column that was only a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:The instrument. A proceeding, in daylight, by the book. A petition and a seat and an answer, and every door in it real law. It cost a great deal and was always lawful, and it was what an office used when it wanted to be seen being right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:55:*Four*, she had said, and struck the floor once with the stick she never leaned on. *Learn them in that order, because they come in that order. And when you have seen the third, gentlemen, do not wait for the fourth to knock. The fourth does not knock.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:That had been the last morning, in a room over a ship's chandler's on a quay, with six young men on benches who were not yet anything, and a cat asleep in the sun on the window-ledge. She had been old even then. She had opened every lesson by telling them that nobody in that room would ever be told the whole of anything and that the sooner they stopped minding the better; she had spent the weeks between on the craft, how to read a subject, how to write a return that was true and useful and no longer than it needed to be, how to live a year in a town and leave nothing behind but a room that had been let. The grades she had kept for the end, so that they would know what the other side of the table did, and where they themselves stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:The boy had had the record since before he could read it. He had had the instrument since the first week of the year, in a sealed packet read out by a woman standing in her own hall. Five days ago he had had the knock, at the gate, in eighty-one words, with a woman standing in the one place in the court that could not be got round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:61:A fourteenth lot was what the knock looked like from inside a ledger. Seln had held its ash on the end of a poker an hour ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:69:He said them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:79:*Nobody has ever protected a man by believing him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:85:A thing kept is a wall. It had been a wall. It had stood between what he knew and whoever would want to know he knew it, and it had kept him safe, perfectly, every day. But it was a wall round the case, not round the boy. The boy was on the other side of it with the rest of the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:The keeper is invisible. He had been invisible for fifteen years. The boy's people had never been invisible for a day. All year they had walked about the hill in the open and let themselves be counted, the girl on the walls with her chalk and the big one at the frames and the researcher with her lamp lit in the top window every night where the whole valley could see it. Five days ago, at the gate, the house itself had stood in the middle of its own court and read the paper out loud. Nobody on that side had hidden anything. They had simply been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:97:He believed him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:99:*Nobody has ever protected a man by believing him.* It was true. He had never seen it done. He was going to try.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:He carried it across the court at dusk, in the rain, with his coat buttoned over the top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:111:He counted them anyway, because he could not help it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:113:A porter with a hand-cart of coal under an oilcloth: one. Two first-years running for the refectory with a coat held over both their heads: two, three. The fire-watch boy with his ladder: four. A Magister of the Ash faculty under an umbrella the size of a cart-wheel, who said, "Filthy, filthy," to nobody: five. The lodge-man's boy, sent across with a lamp: six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:119:The boy's door was the last on the second corridor. Seln knocked once, and opened it when the boy said to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:121:He was at the window, at the sill, with Hesk's book open and the lamp lit and the notice still pinned to the frame. He looked round, and his face did what it always did when a door opened, which was nothing, and then it went still in a different way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:125:He kept one hand flat on the lid and did not sit. The lamp found the brass of the lock and nothing else, because there was nothing else on the outside of it for a lamp to find, and the boy's eyes went to the lock and stayed there a moment and came back up to his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:Seln heard what the boy was offering him. It was a way back down the stair. The boy had built it in two words and held it open, so that a man could take his case and go and nobody need ever mention it again. Seln looked at it, and did not take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:135:"No. That's how you'll know it's true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:They decided the rest in a quarter of an hour, standing, with the case between them on the sill and the rain going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:139:Not here, said Seln. A residence room had a door anybody could knock on, and a corridor full of people who knew whose room it was. Not the wing either. The wing was his, and everybody who watched the wing knew it was his, and a lamp in it after hours was already a thing somebody wrote down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:145:"The researcher's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:147:"It has Bracken's card on the door. *Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.* It has the brass lock and one key, and she wears the key. The old lumber room across the landing has the code on a shelf and the assistant's table, and the house has called the two rooms together the annex since Bracken had it cleared, and people go up and down that stair all day with books." He stopped. "And it's hers. Nobody on the hill would think twice about four people going up to her room. They've been doing it all year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:161:The wing posted the floor schedule every Seventh-day for the week to come: four floors, their hours, and who had booked each hour, in Seln's fair copy, pinned up on the wing's board and the residence board and the refectory door. Everybody on the hill read it. Nobody on the hill read it for anything but their own name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:167:"There'll be six names in the First-day column," he said. "There always are. Ephram has the Crown yard from the second bell every First-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:Bracken saw the chain go across Ostrand bridge a little after the fourth bell on the Fourth-day, and came down from his rooms with the ship's glass still in his hand to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:191:He found Cael on the north wall, because it was where Cael always was at that hour, and stood beside him in the rain without an umbrella and gave him the glass. Cael found the bridge. The bridge-ward was at the near end in his oilskin, with a man from the town, hauling a chain across the roadway from post to post. Below them the river was standing up against the middle pier in a long brown hump, and the iron rings let into the stone were gone under it, all but the top two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:193:"The third ring," said Bracken. "They shut it to everybody at the third. Carts, horses, people on foot. Since the year the middle arch went, which was before my time." He took the glass back. "The ferryman tied up at noon. I watched him do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:197:"The sitting's tomorrow," Bracken agreed. "The seat does not adjourn for weather. I have never known it to, and the house has never in my time asked it to." He looked out at the water for a moment longer. "Come and see the coachman. He's in the stable passage with a map and an opinion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:199:The house's coachman had driven the delegation coach for as long as Cael had been on the hill. He was a short broad man with grey whiskers who talked to his horses in a low steady voice all day and to people hardly at all. He had a road map of the valley spread on an upturned bucket in the stable passage, weighted at the corners with four horseshoes, and he put one thick finger on it when they came in, as if he had been waiting with it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:201:"Up this side," he said. "West, along the bank road. Two hours if the bank road holds, and it will, it's high. To the upper ferry at the narrows." The finger moved. "She runs in any water. Rock both sides, no width for the river to throw her about in. Over there. Then back east down the river road on the far side, two hours more, into the city by the west gate." He looked up at Bracken. "Leave at the hour before light. In by noon. Sitting's after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:209:"Since the gate, no enrollee of this house leaves the hill alone," she said. "You know that. Tomorrow you are five, and the house's counsel went down to her rooms by the wharf this afternoon while the bridge was still open, and will meet you at the side door." She looked along the table at each of them, the way she looked at a page before she signed it. "I would go with you, if the seat had asked for me. It has not. It has asked for him, and the maker of the copies, and the advocate of record. Go the long way and come back the short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:She went out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:217:He came up to Cael's room an hour later with his kit bag, all the same, and turned it out on the bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:219:It was what he took to every floor that was not the house's own. Two rolls of linen strapping, a roll of the stiff kind that the healers used for wrists, a tin of the porter's horse salve that smelled of tar and worked better than anything the infirmary had, a clean neckcloth, a flask, a heel of bread wrapped in a napkin, and a short length of leather with a buckle at each end that Cael had never seen him use and had never asked about. He went through all of it with his big careful fingers, as he did before a meet, and put it back in a different order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:225:She did not laugh at him. That was how Cael knew she had been thinking about the road too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:Karis came last, with the attestation wallet under her arm and her coat already over the back of the chair as if she meant to sleep in it. She checked the wallet's contents leaf against its contents, which she had done at supper and would do again in the coach, and then she sat on the end of the bed beside Brom's kit and looked at the sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:229:There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:239:"If you'll have it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:241:"He asked for my room." She said it slowly, as if she were reading it off a page in a hand she did not know. "Out of every room on the hill." Then she looked up, and Cael saw that she was not frightened, or not only. "Yes. Tell him yes. Tell him I'll have the girl sent home early that day, and the kettle on, and every paper on the table turned over." She stopped. "No. Don't tell him the last part. I'll just do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:247:"Two years he's sat at the middle of the bench," she said. "Two years, and never once put anything of his on the table. Not a cup. Not a word he didn't have to." She turned round. "Good. That's all. Good."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:249:Brom stood and shouldered his kit bag. At the door he stopped, and put his hand flat to the left side of his coat, where the letter was, the one he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day, and kept it there a moment, as if he were checking that the road had not already taken something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:255:*The bridge chained at the third ring. The ferry tied up. One road to the seat tomorrow, round by the upper ferry, four hours. Seln came up the stair at dusk with a case under his coat and set it on my sill. He's going to open it. He isn't sure. I believe him more because he isn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:3:The minute was exactly as long as Norhold's, and nothing like it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:At Norhold the wrong thing had answered. Here nothing answered at all. He knelt in the dust and called his own legs, out of habit, to get him up, and the calling went out of him and found nobody at home. The Wind did not lift its head. The read lay where he had left it. Everything he carried had turned, all together and very politely, to look at the new thing standing in the doorway, and none of them was listening to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:Brom stood over him with his left arm hanging and his right hand open at his side, and did not say anything, and did not touch him. He stood the way he had stood in the channel. Anybody coming down the road would have had to come through him first. Nobody came. The wind went along the cut and the horses blew and stamped and the coachman talked to them. Somewhere down the bank the river roared through the narrows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:11:Then, one by one, the things he carried turned round again and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:13:The read came first, as it always did. Then the Wind, a stir low in the belly. Then the rest. He called his legs and they came. He got one foot under him, and then the other, and Brom put out his right hand and Cael took it and came up off the chalk onto his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:21:He sat down on the lip of the south bank above the hollow, where she had come out of the shadow, because his legs had decided that for him. The chalk was cold through his clothes. Below him on the river side the hill fell away through hawthorn and willow to the brown water, and he could see a long way down the valley toward the city, the flat land shining with floodwater in long strips, and a smudge of smoke on the far edge of it that was Ostrand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:23:It had been waiting for him since he held still on his knees, as it always waited. It came to the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, and stayed there, patient as a clerk, until he had a moment to look. He looked now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:34:He read it through twice. Then he counted, because he could not help it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:38:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:52:Brom was on his knees in the dust at the edge of the north ditch with Lira's foot in his lap. He had the kit bag open beside him, the one Lira had laughed at the night before, and he was strapping the ankle one-handed, with the linen held in his teeth. His left arm hung. Every time he needed to pull the strapping tight he did it with the right hand and his knee, and he did not once ask anybody to help him. Lira lay back on her elbows and watched him do it, white to the mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:56:Nobody had asked her. She said it to the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:58:"Four bursts. On chalk. That's the road's whole free budget and not one over it. I counted." She shut her eyes. "The ankle wasn't the bursts. The ankle was the ditch. I came down on the edge of the camber where it falls off, and the edge went." She opened them again. "Write it down that way. I want it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:62:"Good." She looked at Brom's arm. "And that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:64:"Shoulder," said Brom, round the strapping. "Left. It's in. It was out for a heartbeat and it went back in by itself when he came off me." He tied the knot with his teeth and his right hand. "A week. It's had worse." Then he sat back on his heels and, with the same right hand, without thinking about it, reached across under his coat and felt the left inside pocket, where the sealed letter was. Cael watched his face as he found it there. Then Brom took the hand away and put it on Lira's strapped foot, flat, and left it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:74:The coachman had got down off his box. He was walking the near horse up and down the road between the coach and the cart, slowly, with his hand on its neck, talking to it. The horse's foreleg had been held by a point for most of the fight. It was trembling. The coachman walked it, and talked, and did not look at any of them. Once, passing the bank where Cael sat, he said without stopping, "Twenty-two years I've driven this coach," in exactly the tone he used for the horses, and walked on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:78:They had come for him. Every one of the six had been there for him, and no one else. The hobbles were for him and the hood was for him and the wagon in the gully, wherever it was, had a door that was meant to shut on him. And the four people he had come here with had paid for every yard of the road. Lira had paid in an ankle, Brom in a shoulder, Karis in a hand she would have to write with for a week. Even the coachman's horse had paid, standing there trembling with the coachman's hand on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:80:He had paid in an arm and some ribs. He had also been given a thing that a Silver had spent her life on. He did not think that came out even.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:82:He did not know yet what to do with that. He put it away, carefully, with the other things, where he would be able to find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:86:Nobody saw him come. One moment the gully beyond the culvert was empty, a cleft of wet stone with the stream running white down it, and the next Seln was standing on the parapet beside Karis with water to his knees and his coat dark with it. He had a long iron pin in his right hand, the length of a man's forearm and thicker than a thumb, with a ring through the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:88:He looked round the cut once, at all of them. At Lira's foot in Brom's lap. At Brom's arm. At Karis's wrapped hand and the word in her notebook. At the coachman walking the horse. At Cael on the bank, with the left sleeve of his coat torn open from the wrist to the elbow and the arm under it raw and white with chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:94:"The off-side one." Seln looked at it as if it were a thing he had found on a beach. "I was under the wagon when they came back down the gully. They walked past it. Nobody looked under it. Nobody looks under a wagon." He turned and dropped the pin into the ditch on the north side, where it went into the mud without a sound and did not come up. "I didn't see anything up here. The bank was in the way. I heard it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:106:Cael watched him go. All morning, from the ferry landing to now, Seln had not been in a single place where anybody on the road could see him. He had not been in a single place where the team could see him. He had not been in a single place where Cael could see him either, and Cael had looked, once, during the third exchange, down the bank toward the willows, and found nothing but willows. The six had come to this road expecting five people and a coachman. They had counted four. The fifth had been underneath their wagon with his hand on its pin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:112:The leather was warm from his body. There was a small mark on the cover, over the place where the book lay against his ribs, a faint dull patch in the grain as if a thumb had pressed there for a long time. He looked at it and knew what had made it. He did not try to rub it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:116:*Eight. Taken from someone the system sent namelessly, to hold me namelessly. No paper. No seal. No office to write to, not even a false one this time. She stood in the road and did the best thing she knew how to do, and I took it off her, and she'll go home tonight to wherever they keep her and nobody will ask her how her day was. A hand and no face. I don't know what I owe someone I can't find. I don't know if owing is the word. Write it down anyway.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:122:*The bills: Lira, the right ankle (the ditch, not the bursts; she wants it written that way). Brom, the left shoulder, out and in. Karis, the right hand, the fourth. Me, the left forearm, the right ribs. The coachman's near horse. Seln, nothing anyone saw. The leaf said Brom for the minute. Brom was there for the minute.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:126:The coachman had the horses back in the traces. Brom lifted Lira into the coach in his one good arm as if she weighed nothing, and set her on the seat with her foot up on the record cases, and got in after her. Karis climbed in holding her hand up. Seln shut the door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:130:It took them a quarter of an hour. The wheel had not broken. It had been unpinned and laid down, and it went back on in two minutes, and then the coachman put his shoulder to the cart and ran it back off the crown into the gully mouth beside the culvert, where it stood beside nothing. Cael did not look down the gully. He knew what was down there. There was a wagon with a bench in it and two rings in its floor, and no pin. Seln climbed up onto the box beside the coachman and sat there in his wet coat, looking down the road toward the city, and Cael got in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:136:The first one came a mile short of the city, at the toll-bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:138:The bar was a long white pole on a pivot across the road, where the river road came in off the flats to the west gate. The coachman stopped for the keeper to raise it. The keeper came out of his box with his hand out for the toll, and the bar went up, and swung, and came to rest against its post. And as it touched the post, something in Cael said, very quietly and with great courtesy, *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:142:Then he did. It was asking whether he would like the bar fixed there, against its post, where it had come to rest. It would be no trouble, and it was a good place. The bar was resting against a surface, and the new thing in him knew exactly how to set a point against a surface and tell it to stay, and it was offering, as politely as a clerk at the elbow, to do so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:146:The offer went away at once, without argument, the way a clerk goes away when you shake your head. The keeper took his toll and the coach went under the bar and on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:148:He sat back against the seat and listened to himself, and found it was not gone. It was quiet, and waiting, and it had found out that he could hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:150:The west gate. The long street of warehouses by the river, every cellar door open and men with buckets. The market street, steep and wet. The four steps of the wool exchange and the side door in its flank. The coachman took them round into the exchange's yard by the stable arch, so that they would not have to get down in front of the steps, and drew up under the wall, and set the brake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:172:"Think how it reads," she said. "Not how it happened. How it reads, on a minute, at a seat, in that room. The respondent arrives at a documentary sitting the seat asked for him in person, with chalk to his knees and his advocate's hand in a wet cloth. He says that on the way here, this morning, on the only road open, six people he cannot name, whose faces he did not see, with no paper and no seal and no office, tried to take him into custody, and failed, and left. His witnesses are the four people who came with him and a coachman in the house's pay." She stopped. "No faces. No names. No paper. No wagon, by tonight, I'd wager, if anybody went to look. On the morning of a sitting, six days after the house turned away an order at its gate and made the order look sinister." She did turn then, and looked at all of them. "It reads as manufactured theater. Jent wouldn't even have to say so. He'd be kind about it. Everybody in the room would be kind about it, and every weekly in four towns would print it by the Fourth-day under a woodcut of the gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:176:The clock across the yard moved its hand on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:178:"She's right," said Brom at last. He said it to his own knees, heavily. "I hate it. She's right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:182:He had not said anything yet. He had been sitting with his arm across his knees and his ribs telling him about the eight feet of lane he had ridden into a point, and listening, and he found that he had known how it would come out before Karis had finished her second sentence. He did not like knowing it. He knew it anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:188:The clock stood at the hour less one minute. Four minutes, from *We tell them* to the nod. He had counted them without meaning to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:196:Then he wrote the second, and the third, and did not ask anything. Cael watched him decide not to. It was the most courteous thing anybody did all afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:210:At the records table Havel sat in the junior chair with the times and the receipts, and wrote the minute they came in. He did not look up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:222:The clerk wrote it. The hood did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:226:Cael stood. His ribs told him about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:232:That was all it asked. It did not ask whether they were all of him. Nobody had ever asked that, and the form had no line for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:236:Karis went up to the clerk's table and signed the receipt leaf with her left hand. Cael watched the pen go. Her name came out wide and slanting and unlike itself. She looked at it on the page for a moment, and did not try to make it neater, and gave the pen back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:240:Jent had sat at his table through all of it with his spectacles on their black cord and his travelling inkwell shut, and said nothing, because a documentary sitting gave the petitioner nothing to say. As the long room emptied he gathered his single folder and came along behind their table toward the door, and stopped by Cael's chair for a moment, and looked at the chalk on Cael's sleeve and on Lira's strapped boot and on all their knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:248:He did it out of habit, the way he had read Jent at the conference and at every sitting since, four times, and found nothing four times. He found nothing now. The man stood there in his brown coat with the mended cuffs, middle height and middle years, with a pleasant remark about the weather on his face, and there was nothing on him at all. There was no weight laid down anywhere, and no grain of hiding. It was not that Jent was hiding what he knew. It was that there was nobody in the coat for the read to find, only a procurator of record, doing the job he had been sent to do, courteously.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:250:He might have known. He might have had no idea. There was no way, from the outside, to tell which. That was the whole point of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:254:Jent nodded, as if that were the right answer to something, and went out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:256:He stayed in his chair a moment after the others had stood. Brom was helping Lira up. Karis was packing the wallet one-handed. Seln was already at the door. The long room was nearly empty, the clerk sanding his minute, Havel squaring his receipt book to the edge of the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:260:He had believed in it. He saw that now, sitting in it, the way you see the shape of a thing only when you are about to put it down. All year he had believed in this room. Not the hood, and not Jent, but the room: the text on the clerk's table, and the code in its three volumes, and the provisions with their dates in them, and the minute that went up under the hood and came back initialled. He had written it into Hesk's book on the first night of the year in his best hand. *The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed.* He had stood in a dairy with the slate wall behind him and defended it at full strength. He had brought receipts to this room all year, and when the room would not take them he had gone on bringing them, because the room was the system and the system had text.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:270:He got up out of the chair. Havel did not look at him. The clerk did not look at him. He went out of the long room after the others, with the chalk on his knees, into the afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:274:The water was under the second ring by dusk, and the bridge-ward took the chain off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:276:They came home the short way, over Ostrand bridge and along the river street and up the bluff road, as Withrow had told them to, with the lamps lit and the river still loud and brown beside them but lower, much lower, with a long tide-line of sticks and froth on the stones where it had been. At the ferry landing the two coats stood in their places in the last of the light. One of them watched the coach go by. He was not looking at Cael. He was looking at Lira's strapped boot, which she had up on the seat by the window, and he went on looking at it until the coach had passed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:280:She did not interrupt them. Karis told it, in order, and Cael put in the parts Karis had not seen, and Brom said nothing, and Lira said the thing about the ditch. When they were done Withrow stood a long moment with her hands behind her back. Then she went to her desk and took out a small book bound in black cloth that was not the house's book, and opened it, and wrote one line in it, standing, and blotted it, and shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:282:"That is mine," she said, "and nobody reads it. It will stay there until somebody gives me a better place to put it." She looked at Karis's hand, and at Lira's boot. "Go to the infirmary. All of you. Now. That is not a request."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:288:Lira's ankle first. She unwound Brom's strapping, looked at it, said that whoever had done it had done it properly and should have been a healer, and did it again exactly the same way, tighter. Six days, she said, and a crutch, and no floor until Rooke had seen her walk across one without thinking about it. Then Brom's shoulder, which she moved in her two hands in four directions while he looked at the wall, and said had been out and gone back in by itself, which it ought not to have been able to do, and put in a sling. Then Karis's hand, which she held under the lamp for a long time without saying anything, and dressed with something that smelled of honey, and wrapped, and told Karis not to write with for five days, at which Karis only held up her left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:5:The card was pinned at the board's head in Rooke's square capitals, as every card was. Under *LIRA* there were three lines, each with a date and a floor and a name. Rooke did not rub them out. He drew one stroke through each, level and unhurried, from the first letter to the last, the way a clerk strikes an entry he means to leave legible, and at the end of each line he wrote two words in the margin. Cael could not read them from the stair. He did not need to. He knew Rooke's two words for a bout not fought, because he had read them in that margin on other fighters' cards, and had never once expected to read them under hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:9:Rooke stood back and looked at the board for a moment with the chalk in his hand. Then he put the chalk in his pocket and went in to the program floor and shut the door behind him, and Cael heard the bolt go home on the inside, which it never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:13:Lira was at the long table in the refectory with her strapped foot up on the bench and a bowl in front of her that she was not eating. She did not ask whether Rooke had done it. She looked at his face as he lowered himself onto the bench across from her, and nodded, and pushed the bowl an inch further away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:17:"In a minute." She looked at him across the table. She had her elbows on the board and her chin on her two fists, the way she sat when she was about to say something she had been carrying round for a while. "You know what day it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:19:He did. He had known since he woke, with the grey light on the ceiling and the new thing in him quiet, named, asking about nothing. He had lain there and said it to himself, and then not said it to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:"It's the eleventh of Sowing," said Lira. "Hesk told me. Two years ago. I wrote to him from Greyvane to ask, and he wrote back in three lines, and one of them was the date and one of them was *thank you* and the last one said *he won't have told you*." She took her chin off her fists. "You weren't going to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:"There didn't seem to be a place to put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:"There's a place tonight. The reading room, after supper. Karis has said yes and Brom's bringing the kettle and I've asked the kitchen for a honey cake, and they said yes because I didn't tell them who it was for." She picked up her spoon at last. "And I've asked Ephram."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:27:He looked at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:33:He had been seventeen for a year. It had been a long one. He had been seventeen at Norhold on the stone with a healer's fingers in his hand, and seventeen on the road home in a cart, and seventeen behind a bell-frame on a gatehouse roof, and seventeen five days ago on his knees in a chalk cut with a cage of points round him. And now he was not. Nothing in him had changed in the night. Nothing ever did, on a birthday. It was only a line in somebody's register moving down a row, but it was his line, and it had moved, and Lira had said it out loud across a refectory table before anybody else could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:41:Bracken brought it up himself. He did not have to; the porter's shoulder was mending and Seln was doing the sort, and Seln would have put it in Cael's hole with the rest. But Bracken came up the residence stair with it under his arm at the noon bell, and knocked, and held it out in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and did not come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:45:"Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:"Don't thank me. I only carried it up a stair." Bracken looked at him over his spectacles. "Many happy returns of the day, enrollee. I have it in the book. Your file has had your date of birth on its first leaf since the day you came up the hill, and I have never once had cause to say it aloud." He nodded, the small nod he gave a form that had been filled in properly, and went back down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:49:Cael did not open it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:51:He set it on the sill beside Hesk's book and looked at it for a long while. It was wrapped in oilcloth and tied with waxed thread in Hesk's own knot, the one that came undone with a single pull if you knew which end and held like iron if you did not. He knew which end. He did not pull it. Lira had said tonight. He thought he would let it be tonight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:55:The reading room had never had so many people in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:57:It had three chairs and a bench and a window seat and the long table, and Karis's shelves along two walls to the ceiling, and the brass lock on the door with its one key on the cord round her neck, and Bracken's card in its brass frame at eye height outside: *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.* When Cael came up the library stair after supper, slowly, with his ribs, the door was standing open and the lamp was lit and the room was warm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been squared to the table's edge and turned over so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid flat with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:65:Brom's spirit kettle stood on the end of the nearest shelf over its little blue flame, not boiling. Brom himself had the chair by the shelf, with his left arm in its sling across his chest and his long legs out in front of him. Lira had the window seat with her strapped foot up on a stool somebody had carried in from the annex across the landing. Karis stood by the table with her right hand unwrapped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:69:The blisters had gone down. The skin of the fingers was whole again, and pink and new across the tips. But below the new skin, from the tips to the second knuckle, the fingers were a shade paler than the rest of the hand, as if somebody had washed the colour out of them a little, the way linen washes out in the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He stopped in the doorway and stood there with one hand on the frame, a tall loose young man in the cohort's coat with the green-taped captain's file under his arm, because he went nowhere without it. He looked at the four of them. Then he looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:83:He came in. Then he saw the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:89:He took the third chair from the end of the table, and turned it round, all the way, so that its back was to the table and its front was to the window. Then he sat down in it, with his back to every paper in the room, facing Lira on the window seat and the dark glass behind her, and put the captain's file on his knees, and folded his hands on top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:101:Cael watched him do it. He had watched Seln look at rooms for two years, at counters and sittings and in the long room at Ostrand from the one seat that saw every door, and he knew the order Seln looked at things in, because it was always the same. The door first: the brass lock, the frame, the card in its brass holder at eye height outside. Then the windows, both of them, the one over the window seat and the narrow one at the end, and what they looked out on, which was the dark and the top of the library's roof and, a long way below, the river. Then the landing behind him, and the annex door across it, and the stair going down. Then the table, with its field of turned backs. Then the people in it, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:103:He took perhaps a quarter of a minute over it. Nobody hurried him. At the end of it he said, to the room more than to any of them:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:107:He came in and sat down at the middle of the bench along the table's far side, where the turned stacks were thickest, as he sat at the middle of every bench. Karis put a cup of Brom's tea in front of him. The kettle had boiled at last, while nobody was looking at it, which Lira said was the only way it ever did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:109:Seln looked at the cup. Then he put out his hand and turned it, a quarter turn, on the table, the way he had turned his cups for two years at every table on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:111:Cael saw it. He saw Lira see it, and Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:117:They sang, because Lira said that you could not have a birthday without, and nobody else on the hill was going to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:123:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:127:Ephram, with his back to the table, sang it with him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:129:He sang it wrong. He had picked up the tune from the verses before and he came in at the end with great confidence and got the third line of it wrong, cheerfully, all on one note, and Brom stopped singing and opened his eyes and looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:141:She had not said she was going to. She sat on the window seat with her foot on the stool and a plate on her knee and did not eat from it, and when Karis put the knife down she said them out loud, to the room, one after another, in the voice she used to give her figures to a panel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:143:"Next Second-day. The Current girl from Rooke's program, on the east hall's boards, rated. I'd have won it." She stopped, and went on. "The Fifth-day after that. The Ash second-year from the northern house, the one with the long reach. I don't know if I'd have won it. I wanted to find out." She stopped again. "And the lake house's exhibition. Their Blade. The one who beat the Current girl on figures at the confluence. I wanted that one most."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:147:"Three. Not fought. Rooke struck them this morning. They'll go on my sheet as losses, and the sheet will go to the evaluation at the end of the year with three losses on it that I never stood on a floor for." She looked up. "There. Said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:151:Cael found that he could not look away from her. She had not hidden them. He had expected that she would, a little, the way he would have, writing the bill small at the bottom of a page where nobody need read it unless they went looking. She had not written them anywhere. She had said them out loud, in a room full of people, on his birthday, in order, with the dates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:153:"I'd have stood there again," she said, more quietly. "In the cut. Where the man's foot was going to land. I'd stand there tomorrow. I'm not saying them so anybody'll say I shouldn't have. I'm saying them because they're mine and they cost something and I want them said where somebody heard." She picked up the cake at last. "That's all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:163:It was the chart. He had known it existed for a year and had never seen it close. Every figure he had put up on the house's floors since the qualifying year, in order, in Ephram's square careful hand, one to a line, with the date and the floor and the three cards and the figure, and a little dot beside each one on a running scale in the margin, so that the dots made a line down the three pages. The line breathed. It went up a little and down a little and never once settled, and never once went past the mark at the top that Ephram had ruled across all three pages in red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:165:At the bottom of the third page, under the last figure, there was a gap. And in the gap, in the same square hand, small: *Excused (ribs).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:167:"Ninety-one," said Ephram, to the window. "Ninety-one entries since the qualifying year, and one gap this week." He was quiet a moment. "I'm not asking about any of them. I've never asked and I'm not going to start tonight." He held out his hand over his shoulder again for the sheets. "I wanted you to know somebody's been paying attention. That's all. Many happy returns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:179:He knew what it was for before he had measured it with his eyes. He took Hesk's book out of his coat and slid it in. It went all the way down without being pushed and stood there with a quarter-inch to spare on every side, as if the pocket had been cut round it. Hesk had not seen the book since the day he bound it. He had cut that pocket from memory, at the far end of the country, and it fitted to a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:Only then did Cael look at the rest. The flap came down over everything. The strap was long enough to cross a man's body and ride on the opposite hip, so that the bag would sit still when he walked and not swing when he ran. And the buckle was not a bought buckle. Hesk's file marks were on the inside of the tongue, where only somebody who had slept in the shavings under that bench would think to look, and the frame had the little bevel Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth. He ran his thumb over the bevel. The leather round it was the heavy brown kind Hesk kept for instrument cases and would not waste on anything that did not deserve it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:185:*Eighteen. A man's tools should fit the road he's actually on, not the one they promised him. Your grandmother would say I'm being unsubtle. She'd be right. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:187:He did not read it aloud. Nobody asked him to. He folded it small again and put it in the satchel's pocket with the book, against the book's back board, and buckled the flap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:189:Across the table Seln was looking at the satchel. He had not looked at anything else since the brown paper came off. He did not say anything about it. He looked at the strap that went across the body, and the buckle that would not catch, and the flap that came down over everything, and Cael thought, though he could not have said how he knew it, that Seln was thinking about roads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:Lira came down the library stair with Cael, step and crutch, step and crutch, with his good hand under her elbow on the turns and neither of them saying that it was the wrong way round, a girl on a crutch helping a boy with strapped ribs down a stair by letting him help her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:199:At the reading room's door he had felt it ask. He had felt it on every doorway since the road, and this one was a good door, oak, heavy, set square in its frame, the kind of door the new thing wanted most. *There?* He had named it under his breath. *Door. Karis's. It opens.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:201:Lira had heard him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:207:"Every door. Doors most. It wants them." He did not know how else to say it. "I name them and it stops asking. But it goes on wanting them, a little, after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:211:"Then I'll name them too," she said. "When I'm with you. Out loud. So you don't have to do every one." She went down another step. "*Stair*," she said, to the stair, quite seriously. "*It goes down. People walk on it.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:225:*Eighteen. The eleventh. Hesk's satchel on the sill. The book fits it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:239:It was the tenth bell; he had heard it go from the lecture range a minute before. He knew the knock. It was one knock, not loud, the way Seln knocked at the wing's inner door when he wanted the Magisters to know he was there without being asked in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:245:"I read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:247:"I know you did." Seln looked past him, once, at the room: at the sill, and the lamp, and the satchel standing against the cold glass with its flap buckled down. Then he looked back. "I wanted to say it, not leave it on a board. That's all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:249:"Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:251:Seln nodded. He did not say goodnight. He did not say happy birthday, or anything about the cake, or the song, or the bag. He turned and went back along the corridor without a sound, the way he went everywhere, and at the corner he did not look back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:255:He went back to the sill and sat with the book open and the pen in his hand, and the satchel against the glass, and the cold coming through it. He thought of the chalk cut, and the fourteen, and the band of skin between a hood and a scarf. He thought of a case set down on this sill six days ago, beside this book, and a man who was not sure. He thought of a leaf from an order-pad at the far end of the country, and the three words on it that Hesk had now written to him twice, a few weeks apart, about two different things that were perhaps the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:3:Brom brought the floor schedule up with his breakfast, in his head, because he could not carry a tray and a sheet of paper with one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:9:"Gone over to the coming Fourth-day. Five floors, five names, a quarter of an hour apart, the same as before." Brom took a piece of bread off the tray and ate half of it. "And there's a new line against wing three. *Hesk-ward: excused (ribs).* In his fair hand. I watched three people read it and not one of them read anything into it but ribs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:21:"The day after your birthday," said Brom. "Lira will have told him. She tells people things when she thinks it'll save them asking." He looked at the tray. "Eat that. The rota's on the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:23:It was; Cael could see it from the bed. Brom had written it on the back of yesterday's floor schedule, in his heavy square letters, and nailed it to the inside of the door with one nail through the top, at eye height, where nobody coming in could miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:25:*BROM, LIRA, KARIS, TWO HOURS EACH BY THE CLOCK ON THE SILL, AND THEN AGAIN FROM THE TOP.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:39:It had been doing that since the toll-bar. All the way home in the coach, all through the infirmary with its basins and its lamp and the healer's cool hands on his ribs, all the night at the sill. *There?* It never pushed. It never repeated itself once it had been answered. It simply noticed, every time one thing came to rest against another, that here was a surface and here was a thing touching it, and that it knew how to set a point between them and tell it to stay, and it asked whether he would like that done. It was the most polite thing he had ever carried. It was like having a clerk at his elbow who wanted only to be useful and had been told to ask about everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:41:It asked about the door against its frame, and the cup on its saucer on the tray, and the tray on the end of the bed, and the bed's foot where it stood against the wall, and the book on the sill and the lamp on the book and Brom's boot where it rested against the leg of the chair, one after another, each as it came to his notice. *There? There? There?* And he said *no* to every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:43:Each refusal cost him a little, not much, about what it costs to say no to a child who has asked for the fifth time whether it can come too. But they added up, as small things did, and by the third bell his head was tired in a way his body was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:49:"How many times it's asked." She had a sheet of squared paper on the notebook's back board and a pencil in her left hand. "You've been saying *no* under your breath since I came in. I can see your mouth. I want a count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:53:"I don't know. A lot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:55:"Then we'll find out." She drew a line across the top of the squared sheet and wrote the bell beside it. "Every time it asks, lift a finger. Don't say anything. I'll make the marks." She looked at him. "I can't write well with this hand. I can make marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:59:"Forty an hour," she said. "Near enough." She looked at the sheet for a long time without saying anything. "It's courteous, isn't it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:63:"Because you keep nodding when you say no." She turned the pencil over. "You only do that when somebody's being polite to you and you don't want to hurt their feelings."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:67:Lira saw it before he felt it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:79:"Moving." He lay still and let it. It was not the pull of the old days, the wrong thing answering, the coat caught on the nail. Nothing answered him at all; he had not called anything. It was more as if a great iron chest in the cellar of a house had begun, very slowly, to shift itself along the wall to make room for another chest beside it, and he could hear the scrape through the floorboards. "It's making room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:81:She watched the dust run. Then she took a stub of pencil out of her pocket and wrote on her palm, which she had done on the honest floor all year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:85:"*Bill, not flag*," she said, and stopped, and looked at what she had written, and rubbed it out with her thumb. "No. I don't know. It's not answering wrong. It's not answering late. It's not answering at all, it's just moving." She looked at him honestly. "I don't know which. I'll write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:91:It came back in the afternoon and stayed. He lay flat through it and let his ears close and open as they liked, while the grey dust ran and stopped and ran again along the wood and the chest in the cellar went the rest of the way along its wall, slowly, scrape by scrape, until it had found the place it wanted and the air went back to being the weight of air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:93:"Forty minutes," said Karis, from the clock, when it was done; she had come up for her shift at the start of it and had not taken her eyes off the dust. "That's longer than the morning's. I think it's sitting down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:95:It was. It did not move again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:103:He heard Lira on the stair before he saw her. Step, crutch, step, crutch, and the crutch's rubber foot on every tread like a soft knock. She came along the corridor. He heard her stop outside his door, and the door, which was still standing a hand's breadth open against its frame, swing inward as she put the crutch's tip to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:109:He was half asleep. He had said *no* to it perhaps four hundred times since the toll-bar. He began to say it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:113:He felt it go. It went out of him as quick and quiet as a breath, without his leave, without his asking, without his having said anything at all but the first sound of the word *no*. It set a point against the left-hand jamb of the door, at the height of a woman's shoulder, and another against the right-hand jamb, and drew the line between them taut across the opening, and told it to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:115:Lira walked into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:119:Brom caught her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:121:He had come up the stair behind her, back from the infirmary early, and he caught her under the arm with his good right hand from behind before she was halfway down, and held her up, and did not let go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:Cael was off the bed without any memory of getting off it; his ribs would tell him about it a long while afterward. He was standing in the middle of the room looking at a doorway with nothing in it, and Lira's face on the far side of the nothing, white, with her shoulder still pressed against the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:129:He reached for it, because he knew how, because he had done it on the road: he reached for the point to tell it not to stay, and he could not find anywhere to take hold. On the road the point had been against his own coat, and he had been inside the thing it held, and he had let it go from where it touched him. This one was not against him. It was against two pieces of painted pine, eight feet apart, with Lira on the far side of them. He was on the wrong side of it. He could feel it there, taut, and he could not get his hands round it any more than you can untie a knot from the other side of a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Lira had stopped pushing against it. She stood in Brom's arm and put her open hand flat on the nothing in the doorway, at the height of her shoulder, and held it there, and looked at Cael through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:135:"Five," said Brom. "Six. Seven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:137:He did not try again, because he did not know what else to try. He stood in the middle of his room with his hands open and looked at her, and she looked at him, and the doorway held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:139:"Eight. Nine. Ten. Eleven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:143:On twelve it let go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's flat hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:149:Then Brom bent, slowly, with his good arm, and picked up the crutch, and gave it back to her, and Lira took it. She came in over the threshold, step, crutch, step, looking at the floor, and sat down in the chair, and put her foot up on the stool. Her hands were shaking. She folded them in her lap so they would stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:157:"I didn't ask it. I was saying no. I was halfway through saying no." He heard his own voice and did not like it. "It wasn't on the leaf. Nothing on the leaf said it would do anything I didn't tell it to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:161:Lira had her hands folded in her lap. She unfolded them and looked at them, and folded them again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:163:"I saw your face," she said. "Through it. The whole twelve. You weren't doing it." She said it carefully, as if she were giving a figure to a panel and wanted it written down right. "You were trying to take it off me from the wrong side and you couldn't, and you were frightened, and it wasn't you. I'm telling you because you'll want to put it in the book the other way round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:165:He would have. He knew it as she said it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:167:"It was mine," he said. "Whatever did it. It came out of me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:"So does your Wind," said Lira, "and nobody blames you when it knocks a tray off a table on the oak." She put her foot more squarely on the stool. "Write that it was in you and it wasn't you. Both. They're both true, and I was the one in the doorway, so I get to say which goes first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:173:Brom told her, and she listened. Then she came in, and sat on the floor with her back to the wall, and drew a line on the squared paper at the bell, and wrote beside it, in her wide crooked left-handed letters, *Doorway. Uncommanded. 12 s. B. counting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:175:She did not say anything about it. She looked at the line for a long time, and then she drew a small box round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:179:He stood in the doorway with his hands in his coat pockets and did not come in. He looked at Lira's foot on the stool and at Brom's sling and at the strapping round Cael's ribs where his shirt was open, and at the rota nailed to the inside of the door. He read the rota. Cael watched him read it, all of it, down to *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.*, and then look at the doorway he was standing in, as if it had told him something he did not have words for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:189:"The ankle's six days in strapping," he said. "I've talked to the healer. You're off the frames and off the floor until I see you walk across the east hall without thinking about it. That's three bouts on the card. The Second-day after next, the Fifth-day after that, and the lake house's exhibition." He took one hand out of his pocket and moved it, palm down, flat, as if he were wiping a board. "I'll strike them in the morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:193:"Three. They'll go on your sheet as not fought. That's a loss, on a program sheet. I won't pretend it isn't." He looked at her a moment longer. "It's a good ankle to lose them for. You were standing where a man's foot was going to land. Brom told me." He put his hand back in his pocket. "I'd have stood there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:197:Lira sat in the chair for a long while after he had gone, with her hands in her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:203:He sat in the chair with his sling across his chest and his feet stretched out and his chin down, and Cael thought he was dozing. The lamp was low. The new thing asked about the cup, and the lamp, and the rota's nail in the door, and the door against its frame, and Cael said *no* to each one, aloud now, very quietly, because he had found that it was easier aloud. Every time a thing came to rest against a door, his whole body went cold and listened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:211:"The leaf. I sat at the sill with all of you on the Second-day and priced it. I wrote down everything I could think of, the first minute and a week without the band and the lane and the read and somebody in the room. I left blanks where I didn't know, the way Seln said." He looked at the door. "There wasn't a line for that. There wasn't a line that said it might do something on its own, in a doorway, to Lira, with me lying here saying *no*. I didn't think of it. I measured it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:219:"At the back of the estate, past the kitchen gardens, in a shed with a dirt floor. Not the estate's forge. His. Mother's people had a smith for the horses and the gates and the real work. My father had a shed with a dirt floor and a little forge he'd built himself, because he liked iron, and he liked a thing you could make with your hands that came out the same every time." Brom moved his feet. "He'd let me in when I was small, if I sat on the bench and didn't touch anything. I sat on that bench a lot of afternoons."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:"Hinges. Brackets. Hooks for the stable. Nails, once, a whole keg, for no reason I ever found out." Brom almost smiled. "He'd measure the stock before it went in. Always. On the dirt floor, cold, with a steel rule and a bit of chalk, three times, and mark it. How long, how thick, where it was going to bend. He'd take as long as it needed. Then it went in the fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:227:"And sometimes it came out of the fire and did something he hadn't measured. It cracked along a grain he couldn't see, or bent where it shouldn't. Once a whole bar went soft at one end and he never knew why. And he'd stand there with it in the tongs and look at it, and he never once went back to the rule and said the rule was wrong. The rule was right. He'd measured it right. The fire does what fire does. Nobody blames the rule for the fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:249:The new thing asked about it. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:251:He had no more *no* in him. He turned his head on the pillow and looked at the latch in the grey light, a curl of black iron with a thumb-piece worn bright by a hundred years of hands, and he did with it what Hesk had done with the scriber and the dividers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:253:*Latch*, he told it. *You lift it with your thumb, and the window opens.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:257:He lay still and listened for it to start again, the way you listen after a dog has barked once in the night. It did not. Refusing had always sent it off like a clerk with his paper still in his hand, not offended, coming back at the next counter. This was not that. This was the clerk reading the answer on the form, and blotting it, and filing it, and going home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:259:He tried the door next, cautiously, because the door was the one he was afraid of. *Door. You open it, and somebody you know comes in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:263:The cup on the tray that nobody had carried down. *Cup, for tea.* The lamp with its blue bead. *Lamp.* The book under it, the chair, the nail in the rota, the stool. And Lira's strapped foot resting on the stool, and the cold went through him from his scalp to his heels, because it had not once crossed his mind that the thing would ask about a person, and it asked, softly, as politely as it had asked about the cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:265:*Lira*, he told it, at once. *That's Lira. Leave her be.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:267:It left her be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:271:He priced it, lying there, because that was what he did with anything new. A word a thing. A word cost almost nothing; a word was cheaper than the Wind's landing beat or the read's quiet minute after. But this house alone had more doors in it than he could count from his bed, and there were other houses, and roads with gates on them, and inns, and every door was going to need its word. It would never be paid off. It would be a small coin he put down at every threshold he ever crossed, so many times a day that one morning he would look up and find he had stopped noticing his hand go to his pocket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:279:"It's a door," he said. "I told it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:286:The marks went down the sheet in their rows: forty in the first hour she had counted, then thirty-eight, thirty-six, thirty-one after the storm moved, thirty, twenty-eight. A line at the doorway with a box round it. Twenty-two through the night, by Brom's count and Lira's, which they had kept on the backs of their hands. Then the dawn, and the naming, and the counts dropped off the bottom of the page all at once: nine, four, two, one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:288:She joined the points with the pencil, slowly, and looked at what she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:294:"I said nine weeks and I said I didn't believe it." She turned back through the notebook to the page on the Second-day, the leaf they had all initialled, where *about nine weeks, Karis's curve, not believed* stood in his own hand. She laid the squared sheet beside it. Then she turned back further, to the column she had kept for the storm since Norhold, and further, to the old figures she had copied out of the old volume, the little points low on the paper from the Wind and the read and the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:302:*For now.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:304:"I don't trust a curve with this few points in it," she said. "I don't trust a curve that only goes one way. Somewhere there's a weight where it turns round and comes back up, and I haven't got a point anywhere near it." She tapped the box. "Until I have, it's two words. And they stay in a box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:310:"The district seat," he said. "Their answer. It came up in the noon bag." He read it out, as he read everything the house received, flat and complete. Two sentences. The office was constituted on the date entered in the district's register of offices, by instrument filed at the seat under seal. The registrar was respectfully referred to the issuing authority of the said instrument for any further particulars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:314:"It's exactly what she said it would say," said Bracken. He folded it once. "So I shall write to the issuing authority of the said instrument. I've begun the letter." He looked at Karis over his spectacles. "You had it a week early, Advocate. I've written that down too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:320:*Priced at Silver, and paid at Silver. Priced: the first minute, with Brom standing over me; paid exactly that. Priced: about nine weeks, which Karis didn't believe; paid two days to the idle state, and the idle state is naming. Priced: a week without the band, which I'm paying now. Priced: the lane and the read unsafe, which I haven't tried.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:322:*Not on the leaf: the doorway. Twelve seconds, uncommanded, with Lira in it. Brom says that's the fire's and not the rule's. I'm writing it down so the next leaf has a line for doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:3:Bracken wound the counter clock every Seventh-day before the first bell, and nobody had ever been allowed to talk to him while he did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:The records hall was dark except for the one lamp on the counter. Rain had not come yet, but it was in the air, and the tall windows along the east side showed nothing but grey. Bracken stood on the little step he kept under the counter for the purpose, with the clock's glass door open and the key in his hand, and turned it eleven times. He always turned it eleven times. Seln had counted it on perhaps eighty Seventh-days and had never once heard a twelfth. Then the registrar shut the glass, stepped down, put the step away with his foot, and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:15:"Registrar." He had his hands in his coat pockets. He took them out. "One question, if you have a minute for it. It's about the paper at the gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:Bracken's spectacles came down off his forehead onto his nose. He did not say anything, which was his way of saying yes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:21:He had chosen the words in the night, and he had chosen them so that there was only one way to answer them. Bracken was a man who answered the question that was asked. You did not have to tell him why you wanted something. You only had to ask it narrowly enough that the answer could not wander off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:Bracken took a moment. He did not look at anything while he took it. Then he put two fingers on the counter, side by side, as if the sheet were lying there under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:25:"Low," he said. "A sixteenth of an inch, against our own stock. I laid the rule on it in the lodge window." He moved the fingers a fraction. "Everything that comes up the hill from the south mill carries the mark the same distance from the deckle. I've put a rule on perhaps four thousand sheets. That one sat a sixteenth low."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:"Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:31:"That's the right thing to make of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Bracken looked at him over the spectacles for a little while. Seln let him look. Then the registrar nodded once, the small nod he gave to a form that had been filled in properly, and opened the day-book, and dipped his pen. He did not ask why. In two years he had never once asked Seln why about anything, and Seln had never once stopped being grateful for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:He had sample leaves of the registry's stock pressed flat in the back of his second manual, one from every posting, because he copied things. He took them out when he reached the copying table, and held the newest to the window. The mark sat where it had always sat. He put the leaves away again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:He had the shape now. He did not have the answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:There were four favours owed to him in the world, and he had never spent one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:53:He knew exactly where each of them was. A man in his trade kept that list the way a carter keeps his water barrels, full and lashed down and never once opened on the easy stretches of the road, because the one time you need water is the one time there is none to be had. A favour was not money. It could not be lent or broken into change. You spent it whole, once, and afterward the person who had owed it knew something about you that they had not known before: that you had wanted something enough to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:57:He went down them in his head, as he went down them perhaps once a season, to be sure they were still where he had left them. A man who kept a mould room at a paper mill in the south. A woman who kept ledgers in a store-house in the west. A ferry-master on a coast he would never see again, who owed him a crossing and would have given him a boat. And an old officer of his own service, retired to a garden, who owed him the largest thing of the four and would have paid it gladly and been ruined by paying it. That one he would never spend. He had known that since the day it was owed. Some debts are only worth keeping so that the other man can sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:59:Two, then, the mill and the ledger, because they were the only two who could answer the only two questions he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:61:He sat at the copying table with the wing empty round him and the rain beginning at last against the windows, and he wrote two of them out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:63:The first went to a man who kept the mould room at the south mill. Eleven years ago Seln had filed one true sentence about him instead of a sentence that would have been more useful to somebody else, and the man had never known which office to thank, but he had known whom. The letter asked one thing. *Was the registry mould mended this year, and if so, when, and was a run made off it after?* It did not say who wanted to know. It did not have to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:67:There was a woman in the registry's western stores who had kept the issue ledgers for the paper that the whole half of the continent wrote its instruments on. Every lot that left her stores went into a ruled book against the office that drew it, with the date and the lot's number and the clerk's initial. She had owed him for nine years. He thought of her at her ledger sometimes, on bad nights, because it was a comforting thought: a person in a room somewhere keeping something perfectly. His letter to her asked one thing too. *The south mill's second run this year: which lots were drawn, and by whom.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:73:At the foot of the road the two coats stood under the eaves of the chandler's warehouse, as close to the wall as they could get. They watched him come down. He let them. He had passed them perhaps two hundred times, and they had written him down perhaps two hundred times as the wing's assistant, with the post. He walked past them at the pace of a man who wanted to be dry and said, "Wet one," and the nearer one said, "It is," and that was all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:79:"It's Seventh-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:"I know what day it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:85:The quick bag went faster than the post by a day each way, and it went to places the post did not. It would cost him a favour of its own, he supposed, one day. Everything did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:He stood a moment in the shop doorway with the twine in his hand, out of the rain, and watched the street. A dray going over the bridge with its load sheeted. Two boys with a dog. A woman under a shawl buying eels from a barrow. The bridge-ward in his oilskin at the near end of the parapet, looking down at the water against the old piers and then at the iron rings let into the stone of the middle one, the way a man looks at a clock he expects to be late. There were five rings on that pier, one above the other. The river was at the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:91:On the way back up he counted the coats again, and stood a moment at the second switchback in the rain with the twine in his pocket, and let them count him back. Two at the road's foot. Two across the water at the landing, where the ferry was still running, though it was running short, with the ferryman leaning on his pole at every crossing to hold her against a current that had not been there yesterday. The Ost was coming up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:On the First-day Karis read the year back to herself, one card at a time, and Cael sat at the end of the long table and listened to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:111:It went on like that, and Cael had not expected it to be hard to listen to, and it was, because he knew every one of those afternoons. He had chosen each figure the night before it was earned, on a page at the back of Hesk's book, and spent the bout earning it to the decimal, and walked off the boards afterward exactly as tired as the rotation had said he would be. Hearing them read out in a row, the whole year so far, in the girl's careful voice, was like hearing somebody read your letters back to you. Every one of them was true. None of them was him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:117:Karis's finger stopped too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:119:"It's different," said the girl. She held the card closer to the lamp. "The copy's got twenty-two and two-thirds. The card's got…" She tilted it. "The card's got it twice. There's twenty-three and a third, and a line through it, and twenty-two and two-thirds written over the top in another ink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:123:There was a note in the margin, so small that you would not have seen it unless you were looking for exactly that, in Seln's square hand. *Figure corrected on the card in the desk clerk's hand, different ink. Corrected figure copied. Both seen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:127:"He found it first," Karis agreed. "Four nights ago, copying straight through the night after the road home. And he copied what the card says now, because that's what it says, and he wrote down that it used to say something else, because it did." She set the card back in its box very gently. "Write it on your slip. *Seventh week: corrected on the original; the maker noted it.* If the seat asks, we show them both, and his margin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:133:"No. It's a correction. A mistake is when nobody wrote it down." Karis turned back to the copy. "Go on. You found it. That's the whole job."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:The girl went on. *Fifth-day, the ninth week.* Cael watched her for a while, and then watched Karis, who had not once looked up at him. She did not need to; the year was in her hands, and she was reading it straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:147:He had copied it out of Karis's notebook on the night of the fourth sitting, because the seat's notice had gone into the house's book and he wanted it somewhere he did not have to ask for. *In person.* Every other sitting of the year had been content to have him on its door log. This one wanted his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:149:It had been raining for three days. It came against the glass in long soft gusts and ran down it, and beyond the glass the court was all grey water, and the fountain was a noise in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:151:Lira was on his bed with her boots off and her back against the wall and her feet crossed at the ankle. She had come in without knocking, as she always did, and sat down without asking, as she always did. Brom had the chair, which was too small for him, and it had complained when he sat in it, and he had sat in it anyway with great care, the way he did everything to furniture. Karis stood. She had a sheaf of the attestation papers under her arm and had not let go of them since supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:159:"I'm only telling you because you were going to ask." She looked at Cael. "You were going to ask. You've been looking at my back for a week like it was a bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:169:He had thought about how to say it all day, and in the end he said it plainly, because there was no way to say it that would make it smaller. On the night of the gate Lira had written her question in capitals in her notebook and left it lying open in the middle of the table, what do they try next, when paper won't do it, and nobody had answered it. He did not have an answer now either. But there was one thing he could do about the question before it was answered, and that was to price it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:171:"If it's her," he said. "If it's anybody like her. If somebody begins a fight with me, in earnest, the way she would begin one. I want it priced before, not after. I want the whole leaf written tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:173:Karis's arm came down slowly, and the attestation papers with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:The covenant was at the front of Hesk's book. He did not need to turn to it to know what it said, but he turned to it anyway and laid the book open on the sill where they could all see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:185:Three lines, in his best hand, from a long table at Halcenvane, written well over a year ago. *Nothing made to happen. Only inside a fight somebody else begins. The cost on paper first.* And under them, in Karis's wording from the last night at Norhold, with four sets of initials after it: *Never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:193:"No." He had thought about that, too. "But it's a guess, and it's somebody's guess who knows. Somebody keeping her for the hard ones isn't keeping an Iron. And if it's Gold, I'm wrong, and the leaf says so, and I'll know I was wrong before I start." He took the pen. "Silver. Call it Silver. If anybody's got a better guess, I'll write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:195:Nobody had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:201:"It's wanting it." He did not look at any of them. "If I write the price down tonight, I'm saying I'd pay it. That's wanting it. On the stair I didn't want anything. At Norhold I didn't know I wanted it until it was in front of me. This is the first time I've ever sat down at a table and wanted one on purpose, a week ahead, in ink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:205:That was all she said, and he looked up at her. She was standing with the papers against her chest, and she did not look frightened, and she did not look as if she were about to argue him out of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:207:"The rule says the cost on paper first," she said. "It doesn't say you have to not want it. It says you have to know what you want before you're standing in the middle of it, so it can't decide for you." She came to the sill and put one finger on the page beside the covenant's second line. "That's what this one's for. You don't get to want it until she begins. That's the whole difference."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:213:So he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:215:Karis gave him the first figure. She had kept a column for the storm since Norhold, the days and weeks and what had leaned in him and when, and she turned to it in her notebook and laid her palm flat across it while she thought. Eleven weeks at Gold for the flag to clear. The first month the worst. A Silver ought to weigh less, she said; nobody could say how much less, because there was only the one point on her curve that was any good, and one point was not a curve. She drew one anyway, lightly, on the back of an attestation leaf, through the storm and through the ones before it. Each of the earlier ones had its bill written in the old volume, a day or two of quiet in some room while the rest of him moved over to make space, and she knew those bills better than he did, because she had copied them. They were small points, low on the paper, close together. The storm stood up at the far side of the leaf by itself, like a tree in a field, and her pencil went from the little ones up to it in a long uneasy curve that she did not like and said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:217:"Nine weeks," she said. "If I had to put a number on it. Something less than a flag, for about nine weeks. I don't believe it. Write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:223:"At Norhold the wrong thing answered," he said. "In the middle of the floor. I called the Wind and something else came, and I went down. If I reach in the middle of a fight, that minute's going to happen again, or something like it. I won't be standing up for it. I may not know which way is down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:227:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:229:"Then that's me." He said it without moving in the chair, in the voice he used to tell the kitchen how many loaves. "Write it. *Brom, for the minute.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:233:"There was a rule at Norhold," he said. "Nobody over the cap."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:235:"There was." Brom looked at the window, where the rain was. "I've thought about that rule more than you'd credit. It was a good rule. It kept a hall full of people off a floor where a Gold was working. I'd keep it again tomorrow, in that hall." He brought his eyes back. "There won't be a cap. Wherever this is, if it's anywhere, there won't be a rail or a referee or a man with his arms crossed over his head. There'll be you on the ground for a minute, and whoever's standing nearest. I'm asking to be the one standing nearest. Write it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:237:He wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:241:Then he came to the part he could not price, and he did what Seln had told him on the oak. He left it blank where he would see it. He wrote three headings down the margin, *Her reach. Her rate. What it costs her.*, and drew a line after each, and put nothing on any of the lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:245:She did not ask. She leaned off the bed and took it, and pulled the book toward her across the sill, and wrote one more line under his in her square capitals, pressing hard, the way she always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:251:He initialled under it. Karis initialled with her small exact letters, and Brom made his mark, which was a B with a bar through it like a gate. Lira put hers last, in capitals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:255:*Priced on the Second-day, before the sitting, with four initials. Three days of rain and the river over the bottom step of the river street. Lira's shoulder: clean, both sides. She saw me counting it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:He was a young man in a wet hat, and he apologised three times on the stair before he got to the door, and twice more inside it. The district seat's records room was a man short, he said. Two men, if you counted the one with the fever. A letter had come in from the registrar of the academy on the bluff, about an order and a seal, and it concerned the proceedings, and the senior records officer had said that the proceedings' own records support was in the town on its days off with nothing to do, and would the junior chair be so good as to check the registers and draft the usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:9:"The usual, sir." The young man held out a packet in oilcloth. "With the senior's compliments. He says there's no hurry before the Fourth-day evening."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:11:Havel took it. He thanked the young man, who apologised again for the rain and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:Inside the oilcloth were two things. The registrar's letter, in a clear upright hand that wasted nothing, asking that the instrument constituting a certain office be supplied, together with the seal under which that instrument was itself issued. And a sheet of thin paper with a pencil rubbing on it of a seal, taken carefully, the grey of the lead built up in even strokes until the device stood out white: the registry's own pattern, the river and the scale, inside a beaded rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:17:He laid the rubbing on the table by the window and looked at it for a long time without touching it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:19:His right hand ached at the web of the thumb, as it had every morning for a year. He opened it and closed it twice under the table, out of habit, though there was nobody in the room to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:29:He did not find the rubbing's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:31:That proved nothing. A facilities office constituted for a proceeding might very well be a continental office and not a district one, and then its die would never have been issued here at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:33:So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:35:The rubbing was not in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:39:Every die the registry used was cut by one of three cutters, and every cutter numbered every die in the beading of its rim, and every number went into the cutters' issue ledger against the office the die was cut for. The ledger itself was in the west. A copy was kept at every district seat, in the strong-room, and brought up to date every quarter by the post. Havel signed for the strong-room key at noon on the Fourth-day, with the clerk as his witness, and went down with a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:41:The ledger ran in numbers, in order, one die to a line, every number cut and every number entered. That was the point of it. He found the place where the rubbing's four figures ought to be, and laid his finger on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:43:The number one below the rubbing's stood there against a die cut for a ferry office on a southern river. The number one above it stood against a die cut for a district fee office in the east. And they stood on consecutive lines, in the same ink, as they had been written, the ferry office's line and then the fee office's line directly under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:45:The rubbing's number belonged between them. There was no line for it. There was no room on the page for one. The ledger stepped over it as if the cutters had never counted it, and the number on the rubbing, by the registry's own book, was a number the registry had never cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:51:The facilities office was in the district's register of offices. He had expected that too. It was entered there in a good clerk's hand: constituted this year, for the proceedings at the seat at Ostrand, by an instrument filed at the district seat under seal. The instrument was in the file. He asked for it, and it came, and he looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:59:Two sentences, and both of them true. The senior read them, nodded, signed, and handed them back for the clerk to copy fair. It would go up the hill with the next bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:The form of the usual had no line for what Havel had found. There was a line for the office and a line for the instrument and a line for where to write next. There was nowhere on it to say that the seal on the instrument was in none of the registers that seals were kept in. Nobody had ever needed such a line. A seal was a thing that was in the registers. That was what made it a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:65:At the inn that evening the river was louder than he had ever heard it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:His window looked down on the bridge. A chain had gone across the near end of it at the fourth bell, he had watched it go from the strong-room stair, and now the bridge-ward's lantern hung on the chain-post and nobody crossed. Below the lantern the water stood up against the middle pier in a smooth brown hump, as if something large were resting underneath it, and went away downstream with a noise like a crowd a long way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:71:He read them through. The first went back to a pump step at Ardenmere and a form of four questions, and to the mark in a boy's file that no office would own. The fifth was a sentence on a senior seat's desk at Norhold, read upside down, closing a door that the first had found open. In between were three more. He read them all twice, slowly, the way the manual said a records officer should read anything he meant to rely on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:77:He read it back. It recorded something that had happened. It did not say what he made of it. That was the rule of the sheets, and he had kept it six times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:A mark with no office behind it. A seal with no register under it. They lay side by side on the table in the lamplight, written years apart, in the same hand. He looked at them for a long while, and he did not make anything of them, because there was nothing in the rule that let him. They were two facts. They were on two sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:85:It was not a decision; he did not decide it. It was more as if he had come to the end of a ledger and found the last page ruled off. Every one of the six recorded something the registers could not answer, and he had always been able to write it down because the registers answered everything else, and he could lay one against the other and see exactly where the gap was. This one had gone through all three registers and come out the other side. There was nothing left to lay a seventh against. A seventh would have to be something he thought, and the sheets did not hold what he thought. They never had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:87:He put the six back between the second manual and the third, the same way round, and buckled the kit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:89:Then he sat at the window with his hands folded, the right one aching, and listened to the river until the bridge-ward's lantern went out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:97:At the foot of the road the two coats under the chandler's eaves stood away from the wall to watch them turn. Cael saw one of them lift his head to read which way. West, along the bank road. The man did not move after that. There was nowhere he needed to go with it at that hour; he would write it on his board and give it to the next man on the bell, and by noon it would be wherever such things went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:103:Seln sat in the far corner with his eyes shut and his hands in his lap. He had sat like that in the coach on every road all year. Cael had long ago stopped thinking it meant he was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:109:"I'm saying it so my mouth knows it before the room does." Karis did not stop turning the wallet's leaves. "The clerk asks the maker whether the copies are his and true. Then he asks the enrollee whether the figures are his own, earned on the house's floors. Then he asks the advocate to sign." She looked across at Cael. "That's all it is. Three questions and a pen. You say *They are mine*, and nothing else, and you sit down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:113:"Like that." She went back to her leaves. "Not a word after it. Whatever you're thinking, don't think it at them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:117:Cael sat with his back to the horses and turned the three words over. They were true. Every figure on every card in Brom's two cases had been earned on the house's floors, by him, on purpose, to the decimal. They were his in the way a rotation page was his, because he had written it the night before. He thought that if the clerk had asked whether they were all of him, he would not have known what to say, and he was glad the form did not ask it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:The bank road ran west along the near side of the river, high on its terrace, with the water on the left below them roaring in the dark. Twice the wheels went into standing water to the hub and the coach lurched, and the coachman talked to the horses until they leaned into it and pulled out. Once a fallen branch lay across the road and Brom got down and dragged it off into the ditch and got back in, wet to the knees, and put the cases back on his lap without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:He did not take the book out. He knew what it said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:131:It was a flat chain ferry, broad enough for one coach and its team and nothing else. A great chain ran from an iron ring sunk in the rock on the near side, down under the water, and up again to a winch-house on the far bank, and the ferry hauled itself along the chain by a wheel turned from its deck. The river came through the narrows deep and fast and brown and very loud. But it could not spread. There was no width for it to throw anything about in. It simply went through, as water goes through a pipe, and the ferry sat on top of it on its chain like a cat on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:133:The ferryman came out of his hut at the coach lamps with a lantern of his own. He was an old man, thin as a rake, in a coat that had been mended so often it was more mending than coat. He looked at the coach, and at the house's mark on its door, and at the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:135:"You'll be the academy," he said. "From up the far end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:139:"Thought you might. Town ferry tied up at noon. Bridge chained at four." He went down the ramp to the deck and stood with one hand on the wheel and looked up at them. "Well, come on. She doesn't mind it. She's never minded it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:141:They led the horses on with their eyes covered, and the coach after them, with all five of them standing on the deck beside it because the coachman said they would ride steadier that way. The ferryman put his weight on the wheel. The chain came up out of the water in front of them dripping and went down again behind, and the deck began to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:147:"Road's good your side," he said, nodding at the far bank without taking his hands off the wheel. "Up the hill and through the cut and down the other side, and then it's all flat to the city. It'll be soup on the flat, mind. But the cut's dry." He gave the wheel another turn. "Only stretch that ever is. It's chalk, the cut. Drinks the rain like a sponge and blows dry in an hour, and you'll come out the far end white as millers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:151:"No trouble." He spat over the side, politely, downstream. "Had a load of hurdles over last evening for the farm on the hill, and that's been my week. You're the most company I've had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:153:On the far landing the coachman led the horses up the ramp and put them to, and they all got back in, all but one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:155:Seln stood on the landing with his collar up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:163:He did not explain. Nobody asked him to. He turned and went down off the end of the landing onto a narrow path among the willows, with the river roaring at his left hand, and in about the time it took Cael to look away and back again he was not a thing that anybody would have looked at twice. He was a grey shape among grey trees. Then he was not even that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:169:The road went up from the landing in two long bends, between hawthorn hedges, and then the hill closed in on both sides of it and they were in the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:173:The road itself was twenty-odd feet wide, cambered high in the middle so the water would run off it, with a ditch along the north side. It was dry, as the ferryman had said it would be. The wind came along the cut from the west behind them and lifted the chalk dust off the crown in little white skirls, and the horses' feet sent it up in clouds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:177:Cael counted the cut out of the window as they went, because he counted everything. Four hundred and forty yards, near enough, by the coachman's pace and the bends at each end. At the far end, the east end, the road narrowed to cross a culvert where a stream came down a gully off the hill and went under the road toward the river, with a low stone parapet on either side. Beside the near parapet stood a milestone. He could not read it from that distance. It would say seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:181:It was an ordinary farm cart with its shafts down on the road and no horse in them, slewed across the crown so that it blocked most of the way. One of its wheels was off and lying flat beside it. There was nobody near it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:183:The coachman pulled up forty yards short and sat looking at it. Then he said something to the horses and put the brake on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:He opened the door and got down into the white dust before anyone could stop him, and walked to the coach's front wheel on the south side, and bent to it as if to look at the hub. Then he went down on one knee on the road and put his palm flat on the chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:193:It went out of him through the hand into the ground the way it went into a floor, the cheapest thing he owned and the one he trusted most. It did not read chalk the way it read a man. It read weight. The road under him was carrying the coach, four tons of it through the wheels, the horses in front, the cart's dead weight across the crown forty yards on. All of that was ordinary, and he knew the shape of ordinary.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:201:Two on the milestone. One on each parapet of the culvert. Three in the north bank, high, where nobody could reach. Two in the south bank's lip. Three in a line across the road behind them, at the west mouth of the cut, where they had come in a minute ago. And three more in the road itself, between the coach and the cart, in a rough curve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:203:Each one was a weight with no body, pressed into the chalk at a single point, as if somebody very heavy were standing there on one toe. The coach had rolled over the three at the west mouth a minute ago without a jolt; they lay flush with the road like stones under the dust, waiting for whatever would be strung between them. They did not move. They did not drain anything, the way Ternhall's anchors had drunk the heat out of Karis's contacts into a floor. They were not strung across the air like the Lattice Path's lines, to be found by walking into them. They were points, and they held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:205:Together they made a frame. He saw its shape as he knelt there, as clearly as he had seen the chalk court on the oak. Both mouths of the cut were closed. The west mouth behind them had three points across it, and the east had the cart and the culvert and the milestone. The north bank was too high. The middle of the road was held. There was one way through the whole cut that the points did not close, and it was a channel along the foot of the south bank, under the overhanging lip, perhaps twelve feet wide, running east past the cart's tail to the culvert.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:He said it to the coach door, not loudly. Karis's face was in the window, and he saw her understand it. He saw Lira, awake now, understand it a beat after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:215:They came all at once and without any noise to speak of. A man stood up from behind the cart, where there had been no man a moment ago, and stood beside its wheel with his hands at his sides. Two more came down the north bank on ropes that had been lying ready in the grass along the top, and dropped into the ditch and stood up out of it. Two came up out of the gully beyond the culvert and walked along the parapet onto the road. Every one of them was in grey road clothes with a hood, and every one had a scarf drawn up over the lower half of the face, so that there was nothing to see of any of them but a band of skin and a pair of eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:217:They carried no blades. Cael saw that at once, because it was the first thing he looked for. They had short staves at their belts, and two of them had coils of soft rope, and the one at the cart had a pair of leather hobbles hanging from his belt, and a hood of dark cloth, folded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:219:And one walked out of the shadow under the south bank's lip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:223:He did not need to see it. He had read her four times across forty feet of stone in the outer court six days ago, and drawn her on the oak in chalk, and written her shape in the notebook with no numbers in it. She had stood on the one level place in the court. She stood on the one level place in the cut. Every line out of the cut went past her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:227:"Stay on the box. Nobody here wants you." He moved his head a fraction, toward the culvert. "The one we want walks east to the culvert. Nobody else is wanted. Nobody else need be touched."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:239:He said it to the three in the coach, and he said it so they would all hear the same thing at the same time. They wanted him whole. They wanted him walking, or carried, but whole. That changed what they would and would not do to him. It did not change what they would do to anyone who stood in the way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:243:He did not hurry. He stepped down into the dust with the two record cases, and set them on the coach step, side by side, squared, and then walked forward along the foot of the south bank, under the lip, into the channel. He stopped in the middle of it. He turned round to face the cart and the culvert and the gully, and set his feet, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:He did not look at Cael. He did not need to. It was one step from there to anywhere a man might want to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:251:"On my count," he said, to his own people, and began it.

