books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-3-Lowmarch on the ninetieth day, from the shelf above the ford, catalogued because it had been twenty days and because the meadow had turned over again while they were on a mountain.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-4-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:5:He counted it before he read it. The river was lower than it had been on the sixtieth, and the ford's poles stood a hand farther out of it; the rope ferry was on the near bank, loading; the smith was working, which mattered, because six marks of what was in the satchel belonged to him. The upper inn had smoke. The lower inn had none. The meadow: forty tents and carts where there had been thirty-one, the grass gone to seed and yellow at the edges, the summer's fires fewer and smaller, and of the thirty-one he could find six. The ash was there. The carter with two mules was not — a woman with a hand-cart of hides had the place — and Brom looked at that, and at the ten paces on, where nothing was, and walked to the ten paces on and put the boxes down, one, two, three, with the right arm doing its share for the first time since the ford. The healer had said a week; Brom had given it seven days to the hour, and picked up the middle box with both hands on the eighth morning, and said nothing about it, and nobody else had either.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-6-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-7-Six days from Oxhollow: a road, and a river, and a road, the ford knee-deep where Teague's crew had walked it quiet, and the five of them a day behind, walking it quiet after them, and not trying to catch them. Lira in two boots again: the right one a carter's, bought at Oxhollow's gate for two marks, a size wrong and packed with the healer's linen. The ankle under it taped every morning. The leg above it new, and aching the right amount, she said, when asked, which was once. Karis with the map and the book, the palms two pink lines each and the pen held as a pen. Seln ahead. And himself: the tape round his ribs where the breath stopped, nine days old and hated by noon every day as she had said it would be; the calf bound in her cloth, and the count on it gone — past twenty on the flat it was a leg, and on the shelf's climb it had said nine, and it was the last thing on him that said anything.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-155-The board's room, with the light gone off the water.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-156-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:157:Six tables. The carters at the two by the road door, five of them, loud, and the hide-woman with them. The middle table: nobody. The best table by the fire: four chairs pushed in and no jug — Teague's crew out on the east road since dawn on the axe-head hold's other slip, the long fence, HOLD, Teague. Pike behind the book at the table by the fire, with a bowl, and the stone on the book. The sheet on the long wall on its four pegs. And the five of them at the table by the road door, because Brom could see both doors from it, with a jug and a loaf, and Karis's book open, and his own satchel flat on his knee.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-158-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-159-He counted the sheet from where he sat before Pike opened the book. Thirteen lines. Theirs was the twelfth. He knew that and counted anyway.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-237-"That's the walk." Brom.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-238-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:239:Cael took the slip from where Seln had pushed it, folded it once along the dust-curve, and put it in the satchel against the leather book, where a thing went that would be taken out later. Then he got up, and went to the fire, and stood at the table by it until Pike looked up from the bowl, and said, "The recovery. The Fallow road. We'll mark it."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-240-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md-241-Pike looked at him for one count of five.
