books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-79-He had it by the rope and the rope did not give and he had a knife and used it, and the box came into his arms heavier than it had any right to be, paper being heavy, and the first animal of the second half came onto the bench a length from the cart.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-80-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:81:Compression wanted him. He felt it want, where it had been wanting since Reydan: take the force, turn it, walk away from it. Anchor wanted the cart, a fixed point under the axle, the cart nailed to the mountain and the boxes with it. He had a hand's breadth of a second to price both. A declaration under a herd that went from declarations, at the one place on the bench where the split was still holding, and the split was Karis's; it stood on her line and nothing else. One more loud thing under the second half, here, and it turned again, and the fin was what it turned toward.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-82-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-83-He held. He took it raw.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-139-The herd: sixteen past the fin in the first half, fourteen in the second, three he had heard go into the gorge and could not swear to. Twenty-seven down the mountain toward the fan.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-140-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:141:The map: in Karis's book, in Karis's satchel, on Karis. Nine narrowings and their widths, and now a tenth, which was not a narrowing. It was an absence. The ledge was gone for a cart's width along three carts' length; what remained was a lip against the bulge that a walker could take with her hands on the rock, and a mule could not, and Oxhollow's carts would not, ever. Seln walked out along the lip in the afternoon and came back at dusk and said it went; that beyond the bulge the bench opened as they had seen it; that for a walker Oxhollow was two days on.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-142-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-143-"And a healer?" Cael said, because it was the sum.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-231-"Hm." The whole of the verdict. She stood.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-232-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:233:He had the log out by then. He had got it out of the satchel with the right hand while she was at Brom's arm, and found that he could hold the pencil if he did not breathe past the place. He wrote one line. It was not his hand. It was a hand that hurt, and it leaned, and it was legible.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-234-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md-235-*Eightieth day. Lira's leg is straight. The boxes are ours. The cart is in the gorge. A healer came up the road because a carter said we were late.*
