books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md-113-Positions, catalogued as he took his own: Karis on the bar, its middle, knife in her right hand and a river stone the size of two fists in her left. Lira at the bar's upstream end, ten paces from Karis, the long knife low. Brom on his stone in the near channel, four paces short of the bar's near end, the current on his left, the bill-hook on its short haft in his right, the road behind him. Cael on the near shingle at the water's edge, twelve paces downstream of where the road went in, nothing in his hands yet and everything in him held. The far channel empty. The pools flat and dark. Seln nowhere he could see.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md-114-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md:115:He held the nine. The Iron-adjacent read wanted the river — the sill through his soles, the weight of whatever lay in the pools — and he did not give it the river. Shadow tried, because it was dusk. Anchor found the bar's line and wanted it. Wind sat where Wind sat, and he put a hand on it, privately, because if anyone spent it tonight it would be him, and told it to wait.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md-116-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-03.md-117-"Now," he said, at the pitch of the road, and Karis lit the point.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-11-The first was a Blade cohort's hand from the academy above Ostrand, the schooled square letters Rooke's yard put into people, with the extra stroke Ephram had always given the last letter of his name. The second was a ledger-keeper's. He had been reading that hand since he was fourteen, entered against his name in a column, and it had not changed: small, upright, forty years old, not one letter leaning.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-12-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:13:He put both in the satchel against the leather book, unopened, because he did not read letters in front of a meadow. Then he put the strap over the shoulder that did not have tape under it, and at first light on the ninety-second the five of them went east off the landing, past the smith and the ash, and onto the road.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-14-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-15----
