books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-63-Once for what it said, which was a sentence about a yard. Once for what it did not say, which was more, and left out on purpose. The third time because it was last. Ephram wrote as he fought, in order, with the exchange that decided it at the end; and a man who had closed a letter on what an instructor had not said had decided that was the thing the letter was for.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-64-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:65:Bracken had told the registry where they had gone. The Line, and nothing past it. He turned that over once and found it was the registry's sheet in his hand, said aloud by a registrar with his hands behind his back: seven dashes, ending in white. He filed it beside Withrow's tenure, which held, and the yard, which was quieter, and put the letter in the satchel against Vell's, still sealed.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-66-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-67-"Well," Lira said. She had come up the bank on the mended leg and stood with her weight on it.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-89-"That's a hand," she said to the carter. "You'll keep it dry and you'll not lift with it for six days, and if you lift with it in five you'll not lie to me about it, because I'll know." The carter took the hand back and went in at the gate. Then she looked up.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-90-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:91:She looked at Lira's feet first, the two boots, and at the leg above the carter's boot, and nodded once at the leg. Brom's forearm, carrying a box by strap and not pulling anything: nothing. Karis's hands, the pen in the healed one: a look, no longer than a count of one. Seln: nothing, because there was nothing on him. Then Cael, and the side he carried the satchel away from, and she looked at the tape through the shirt as if the shirt were not there.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-92-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-93-"Fifteen days," she said. "Since the break."
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-229-*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-230-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:231:He closed the book on it and put it in the satchel against two letters, and that was where it went.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-232-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md-233-They made the camp there, on the near side of the stone, on the road, because Seln said the road; no fire, because Seln said none, and nobody asked him why, because Pike had said where the dogs lived. Brom put the boxes down, one, two, and took the third off the mule and put it down, three, on the Thornwater side of the stone, and left them there. Lira sat with her back to the mule's pannier and did the ankle's tape again in the last light, because that was when it was done. Seln went up the road as far as the stone and no further, and stood there a while looking at the bend, and came back and sat facing the way they had come. Karis wrote. It was not long, and it was not a hypothesis, because he knew the shape of her hand when it was writing one, and this was the shape it had when it was writing down a stride.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-207-*Forty-ninth day. Lowmarch. The lower inn, the table by the road door, a jug we can afford because of nine marks.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-208-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:209:*Inventory, because I do one. Nine confirmed. One anomaly, session nine, still open, still real, patience. Deployment since the Line: none. Shadow tried again at dusk coming up from the landing, and I let it try, and it stopped. Anchor wants this doorway; it has wanted every doorway since the Ostrand road; I am letting it want. Eighteen. No injuries. Eighteen marks earned and about ten left after the jug.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-210-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-211-*The board, as a system, because I would not trust it until I could see what it wanted. Hirers post — holds on good paper with a brand, brokers on thin paper with a clerk, carters in Pike's own hand because they can't write and he can. A crew marks the slip and the job is theirs, unless a hold has named a crew, and holds name the top of the wall. The crew goes and does the thing or doesn't. Whoever took delivery marks the slip and says what happened. No mark, no line. The slip comes back by any hand at all. Pike reads it aloud before it goes in, so the crew can say it's wrong, and reads it aloud after, so it's said — because half the meadow can't read and the book is his and the saying is theirs. Five columns. Then a wall, in his hand, ordered by him, on which a Bronze who lost a carter is under a Copper who brought one home.*
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-217-*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-218-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:219:He read it back once. The sentence was there, in his own hand, plain, and he let it stand, and noticed himself letting it, and did not argue with the noticing either. He closed the log and put it in the satchel and the satchel on his knee, where it sat flat.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-220-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-221-Karis had gone to the wall with the notebook. Pike had not looked up.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-231-He stopped the way a man stops when he has a thing he does every time and has never once skipped it. He did not look at the room. He did not look at the wall, or at Pike, or at the ledger under its stone. He looked at the tall woman, and then at the man with the crossbow, and then at the young one — each, in turn, for the length of a breath, the way Cael counted five at every halt — and only when he had them, all three, at the table with the jug, did he step in off the sill and let the door swing to behind him and begin, unhurried, to read the room.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-232-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:233:Force Path. Cael had it from the doorway, and checked it as the man came in: the weight down through the heels, the shoulders carried low and square, the hands loose — the same setting Pike sat in, except that this one had not stopped using it. Bronze, the book inside had said once, and would still say if anyone opened it, and nobody would. There was nothing on him a registry had given. No token, no cord, no colour. A face that had priced a great many things and kept none of the prices on it. He went to Pike first, and Pike said something short and put a slip in front of him — the ford's, Cael thought, by the size of the paper — and the man did not pick it up. He put two fingers on it, as Pike had put two fingers on the ledger, and left it where it was, and looked at his table, and only when the young one had food in front of him did he look back down at the slip and read it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-234-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md-235-Then he turned, with the slip still under his hand, and read the room properly, and the room included the five of them.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-21-Four days down.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-22-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:23:He wrote that at the first halt, because he had the log and the satchel and nothing else — the first time since the Line that he had come off a job carrying nothing but paper — and the sentence was the shape of the thing he was in. Four days: the bench going down and widening and losing its edge until it was a slope and then a hillside; the shale giving way to turf in patches, and then to turf with shale in it; the gorge opening below into a valley with Oxhollow's river at the bottom of it. Lira on the mule, the bare foot in a sock of Oryn's linen, saying nothing about being on a mule. Brom under a box. Seln under a box, ahead, and then not ahead, and then ahead again. Karis with the packs and the map, drawing the bench down as she had drawn it up. And the healer.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-24-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-25-He catalogued her walk on the first day, because it was the first thing she did in front of him that was not her hands.
--
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-163-She loaded it herself, at the gate, with the sun going off the bench above the roofs. The roll under the strap; the panniers, which had carried a box for four days and now carried the kit. Lira, on two feet with a hand on the stockade, watched her and said nothing; Brom held the mule's lead while she tightened a girth and gave it back without being asked, and she said "Hm," to him, and it was thanks. It was not a leaving that had been arranged. It was a healer at the end of a stop.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-164-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:165:"What do we owe," Cael said. He had the marks in the satchel, and he had priced a leg on the board and did not know what it was worth at a table.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-166-
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md-167-"Oxhollow feeds the mule. That's Oxhollow's." She took the lead, and looked at him, and it was the look from the third reading, the one with no number in it and no awe. "Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
--
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-31-- [x] **Book 7 delivery (power development — the first non-combat ability):** Oryn demonstrates a Tide Path diagnostic technique (the *reading*, defined in the reference section) and Cael absorbs a fragment (Ch13 — the series' Chapter 13 acquisition tradition, B2/B3/B4/B6, honored a fifth time, and for the first time the engagement is *clinical*). He now has, in partial form, the ability to perceive internal energy architecture. He uses it, quietly, to study his own (Ch14, Ch16, Ch22) — and the book's discipline is that what he finds when he looks is *described as sensation and shape, never as explanation*: no word for what the architecture is, no theory of what it predates. The tenth fragment notice is shown in full in the established format, and carries the first *Engagement: clinical* field. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-32-- [x] **Book 7 delivery (the ending, exactly per bible):** The companions locate three more Quieting sites (the Long Stair, Ch20; the Drowned Hall, Ch21–22; the fourth, sighted and paced but not entered, Ch23). The sites are on an alignment — not random. **Someone made this pattern.** Ch24 stages the discovery as the series stages its discoveries: on paper, by Karis, with four marks and a straightedge, and the sentence said once. Who, when, why: NOT approached. The word *Architect* does not appear in this book. The word [UNBOUND] appears in exactly one chapter (see below). ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:33:- [x] **The Tide-adjacent anomaly — CONVERGENCE, NOT RESOLUTION (LOCKED per B6's checkpoint):** Book 2, session nine: a Tide-adjacent reading with no Tide practitioner present, no stakes, unreproduced for five books. This book acquires a *real* Tide-adjacent fragment (Ch13). The convergence is staged with total honesty in Ch14 and Ch16: with the new fragment in hand, Cael can finally compare — and the comparison *deepens* the anomaly instead of closing it. The session-nine reading was not this. It predates any Tide source in his life; it had no engagement, no stakes, no demonstration; and the new fragment, turned on his own architecture, finds something in the place the anomaly logged — a shape that is not the tenth fragment and not any of the other nine. He writes it down as what it is: "Still open. Still real. Now I can *see* it. That's worse." The anomaly's resolution remains reserved far past Arc 2 and is NOT explained by the Quieting, by Oryn, or by anything in this book. The standing three words are retired in favor of the new four, and the log says why. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-34-- [x] **Book 8 plants (two, advanced one notch each, absolutely not resolved):** *(a) The Compact already knows.* At the Fallow Ring (Ch11) and again at the Long Stair (Ch20), the companions find registry survey stakes — numbered, seal-stamped, weathered by roughly five years — set at the perimeter's exact edge. Someone with instruments paced these circles before Karis did, and the Compact's edge-territory maps (Seln's professional memory, Ch12) carry blanks precisely where quiet ground lies. Stated on the page once: the Compact has measured this and filed it somewhere the companions cannot reach. That is the whole advance. The *six years of suppressed Quieting records* Vastin obtains in Book 8 are the payoff; this book only proves the records exist. *(b) The sites are older than the system.* Karis dates the fused-stone floors against every masonry stratum she carries (Ch15) and reaches the limit of her method: older than the registry, older than the standardization directive, older than the two-hundred-year falsification and the sub-layer beneath it — "older than the word they retired." One controlled appearance of [UNBOUND] (Ch15), in her voice, as a *limit* — the same stratum discipline B6 Ch16 established: the sites predate everything she can name, and she cannot say by how much. LOCKED until Book 8. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-35-- [x] **Book 9 plants (two, required by CANON_RULES — earliest plant Book 6, minimum two by reveal):** The Arbiter system is the Architect's infrastructure. *(a)* Every Arbiter in the party goes silent inside quiet ground *identically* — same distance, same instant, same way, regardless of Path, tier, or person; Karis's finding (Ch15) is that a personal spiritual entity should not fail like a piece of equipment on a circuit, and she enters the sentence as an observation she does not know how to file. *(b)* Cael's Arbiter — the sigil that has been dark since a Kindling eleven seconds long — does not change inside the Quiet, because there is nothing to silence (Ch11, Ch22); the log's line is the plant: "Everyone else's went to sleep. Mine has been asleep since I was fourteen. Out here that makes me the only one who's awake." Neither plant is explained. The Arbiter never speaks (Book 11's is the first activation; nothing here anticipates it). ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04 ⚠ 2026-09-04 (secrets audit): plant (b)'s Ch22 citation is wrong — Ch22 contains no Arbiter; the dark-Arbiter log line is Ch11 L249, supported by Ch09 L211–217, Ch10 L93, Ch15 L73–77, Ch08 L121. Book 9 minimum met (Ch15 L65 + Ch11 L249).
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-36-- [x] **Book 11 pre-plant (one, gentle, optional-but-taken):** The Quiet's perimeter is a perfect circle — paced, measured, verified at three sites — and a perfect circle is a *made* shape (Ch15, Ch24). Nothing more. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04 ⚠ 2026-09-04 (secrets audit): 'verified at three sites' is weaker in prose — paced at the Ring (Ch10), corroborated by the kit ledger and the wall-line at the Stair, never measured at the Hall (Ch21 L23); the 'made shape' sentence originates at Ch10 L55, not Ch15. Gentle pre-plant still present.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-37-- [ ] **B6 threads honored at their correct altitude:** *The faceless faction* — reaches across the Line twice, and the book counts both: **first on paper (Ch8)** — a genuine recovery contract for a survey kit the Compact abandoned five years ago, laid on the Lowmarch board through an edge-territory broker in registry prose, which Seln flags as written-from-inside and which the crew takes anyway; its completion report travels back west (Ch16) and tells the poster exactly which crew walks into quiet ground and walks out; **then in person (Ch17–19)** — a second posting from the same broker, priced as a lure, at a site where the poster knows six practitioners will be five bodies and one anomaly; the team that waits is off-channel, nameless, and equipped for practitioners who cannot use their Paths — because the Compact *knows what quiet ground does* (plant a, above, given teeth). The knowledge progression is explicit: the first contract was recovery plus a question; the report answered it; the second contract was the trap. Still unnamed, still unlocated, still open into Book 8+. *The Compact's question about what Seln kept* — the trap is for the case as much as the boy, and Seln says so (Ch18); the case is not taken; the dread stays live. *Asset-restriction, pending jurisdiction* — the reason nobody goes west, stated once (Ch2), and the reason the trap has to come east. *Daeva's rematch / Reydan's answer* — one log line each (Ch14, Ch24), warm, unscheduled, not advanced. *Hesk* — letter beats only, via the Lowmarch courier chain (Ch4, Ch14, Ch24); his history untouched. *Vell* — one letter (Ch24). *Ephram* — one letter, from inside (Ch9), the inside friend reporting the weather. *Vastin* — one window (Ch16), three sentences, still inside, receiving a courtesy copy and writing nothing; his departure and the classified-history material are Book 8's and are not staged, hinted, or scheduled. *Havel, Ilsev, Withrow, Coss* — offstage entirely. ⚠ 2026-09-04: Ch8's posting is not a genuine recovery contract — Ch12 line 179 rules it "For a kit that was never here" — and Vell has two letter beats (inbound Ch9 line 141, outbound Ch24 line 97), not the single Ch24 letter the card allows.
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:38:- [x] **LOCKED — untouched threads:** The patient Iron Skin watcher (B2 Ch15), the Book 1 market stranger, Hesk's full history, Coss's grade, the nameless Anchor operative's "unpayable account" (B6) — the last is touched in one log line (Ch19: the second nameless team, same hand) and not advanced. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
books/book-07-void-roads/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-39-- [x] **NEW open threads (deliberate):** **The fourth site** — sighted, paced, not entered (Ch23): larger than the other three, and the alignment points *through* it; Book 8's on-ramp. **Oryn's route** — the circuit of holds that depend on her, which she has not abandoned and will not (her independent want, Ch7, Ch9, Ch18, Ch23–24); she walks with the crew from Thornwater (Ch9) as a stop on her route, leaves after the Ring to make up the days (Ch16–17), and rejoins on her terms (Ch18); available as pressure in Books 8–9. **The reading, turned outward** — Cael can now perceive other people's architecture at contact range, and the book names the temptation and does not pay it (Ch16, Ch22: the losable moral stake for this book — he reads no one without asking; the one time he is tempted is Teague, and he doesn't). **What Cael saw in himself** — described as shape, logged as unexplained, carried forward (Ch22). **Teague's crew** — the rival independents, priced and honest, available to later edge-territory books. ✔ verified against prose 2026-09-04
