books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:21:He said it flat, looking at the stone behind them, and Cael looked with the Anchor-adjacent perception — which was not a declaration, only a way of seeing, and which the rim could not have seen him use — and saw what Seln had read off the ground: fixed points, blooming, along the perimeter's inside edge north and south of the stone, laid in a course as close-set as Karis's ignitions, closing the meter of ground they had crossed. Not a wall. A door, shutting behind them, laid by somebody on the rim who had the craft and had had an hour.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:95:Six on the rim. A lattice at the stone, laid from the rim by somebody with the craft — not the woman on the Ostrand road; he had felt her points from inside and these were not her hand; the same craft, a different weight in the laying, a different spacing — and the whole of the rim's plan resting on two things it could not see. That inside the line nobody could declare. And that anything that *could* declare inside the line would be seen doing it, because a declaration is loud and the rim was listening for exactly that.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:133:Twenty meters from the stone. Ten. He let the second thing off its hold there and not before — Anchor-adjacent, a perception first, still nothing anyone could see — and the lattice's nearest point came up in it a stride inside the line, the cornerstone of the course that shut the road, and he knew its weight as he knew a floor's: a fixed point laid by a specialist, on the rim's side of the shutting, from ground where her craft worked, holding a meter of dead-Path scrub closed against people who could not have opened it if they had known it was there.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:135:He could open it. He had opened one from inside on a road. The perception turned into a hand, and found the knot where the point was bound to the ground, and let it go, the way the woman on the Ostrand road had let go of a spent position, not broken, not overwhelmed, simply *released* by something standing inside it that had no business knowing where the knot was.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:209:*Inventory, because I do one. Nine confirmed. One anomaly, session nine, still open, still real, patience. Deployment since the Line: none. Shadow tried again at dusk coming up from the landing, and I let it try, and it stopped. Anchor wants this doorway; it has wanted every doorway since the Ostrand road; I am letting it want. Eighteen. No injuries. Eighteen marks earned and about ten left after the jug.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:35:She did the arithmetic before she touched anyone, aloud, because that was how she kept it. Cael sat up in the rut to hear it, and his own bill came up with him: the ribs stopping his breath at the tape as he turned; the right calf gone stiff and hot inside the stuck cloth; the nine gone to nothing with a hollow where each had rung, faint now. Nothing wanted anything, except Anchor, which since the Ostrand road had wanted to fix points in doorways and now wanted the survey stake sixty meters south along the line. He let it want.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:87:"She asked for it. She's asked four times. Nobody asked Ephram to be measured; he asked to measure. Nobody asked the woman on the Ostrand road anything." A pause the length of a man checking a fact against a shelf. "That's not consent. It's the nearest thing to it the log's ever had, and it's still not it. I'd enter both."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-13.md:187:Anchor, which since the Ostrand road had wanted doorways, wanted nothing under her hand at all, and he filed that. Blade. The current went along the ninth and slowed, and slowed, and he felt her feel the edge of it — not the edge it declared; the edge of *it*, the place where the ninth movement should have stopped and become bank — and felt her not find it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:205:*Inventory. Ten confirmed. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected; the seal holds out here as it held inside; used once past the Line, on a floor, in front of the circle and nobody else. Storm-adjacent — Daeva; resettled once this week round the new neighbour, on the second morning, in the yard, and I heard it do it. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; it wanted the stake and it can want. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only non-hostile in the log until three days ago. Tide-adjacent — Oryn. Partial. Iron. Self and contact. Engagement: clinical. Does nothing in a fight, and is the first thing I've ever owned that lets me hear the other nine. Cost: three days of listening; the idle state learned by the third night, and it isn't quiet, it's just that I've stopped putting my hand to every string. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:67:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:141:Pressure-adjacent, because it was loud and he needed it for nothing else. Ember-adjacent, one point held out at arm's length as he ran, Karis's, a bead of white that lit the scrub five paces round and made it go still and then come on harder. Blade-adjacent, Ephram's, which had nothing to cut and rang anyway. Storm-adjacent, Daeva's, the pressure-differential seeded ahead of him so that the air went wrong for ten paces. Anchor-adjacent, let off its hold, looking west. He ran through the middle of his own noise with the Iron read under his soles and the calf hot and the ribs stopping his breath at the tape, and came up out of the scrub onto the rut, and turned east along it, the wrong way. The dog that had cut the road was behind him now, with seven others, after the only declaration they had ever met on their floor.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:167:He saw the lattice before it lit, because Anchor-adjacent was still running and perceived fixed points, and hers were laid along the ground in a row so close they were one thing, as she had laid them across a mountain — along the line's inside edge, the meter of ground between the scrub and the stone that every hound in the circle had crossed every night without once needing to be careful of it. They lay along it as the stake had, to the width of a boot.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md:57:He had never once used it as a trial; he had used it to open a cage and a door, and every other time it had been a want he had told no. Now he put the Anchor-adjacent on the flooded floor five strides out, where the second stone lay, and laid one fixed point there. Not a cage. A single binding, the thing the woman on the Ostrand road had laid four at a time, taken from her and made his: a point fixed *against* the surface it was laid on, so that the surface held it as a nail holds a board — held pressure, a weight that did not sit on the floor but pressed on it and kept pressing, and would keep pressing until he let it go.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md:157:He laid it on the floor. Not on the water; on the *floor*, at the broken centre, a stride from his own boots, where the fused stone ended and the spring came up: one fixed point of held pressure, the binding from the Ostrand road, laid now to stay, against the stone, as a nail in a board, the heaviest thing on the floor by the only arithmetic the floor knew.

